In article <2f6bec0a.0107250034.3f8a3a2e@posting.google.com> banny52@yahoo.com (Ginny E. Kim) writes:
>I got following message while I am willing to debugging kernel..
>
>[/]#dbx -k /vmunix
>dbx version 5.1
>Type 'help' for help.
>
>main:    Source not available
>
>warning: Files compiled -g3: parameter values probably wrong
>(dbx) 
>
>
>How can I do to use -g compiling option?

Unless you have a source license and distribution, you can't.
Most (98% or so) of the kernel is compiled at Compaq and shipped
as object files. There is no way to recompile them with -g.

Also, using -g would increase the space required at least 100%,
and (due to the extra code needed to preserve everything) slow
the kernel down considerably.

-g3 is sufficient for almost all debugging needs. See the documentation
on kernel debugging for more information on how to work around some of
the few issues.

