Steven Hobbs wrote:
[...] 
> I was a member of the original Alpha Architecture design team (then called
> the RISCy VAX design team).  Mulp correctly describes the reason why Alpha
> originally did not have 8- and 16-bit loads/stores.  It was believed that
> future implementations would have write-back level 1 caches and no competent
> hardware implementation engineer would do a write-back without ECC checking.

Thanks for the insight to the original reason. The story apparently
changed to the simplified shift network after Alpha was released (with
a write through cache). For example, in Richard Sites's decription of
Alpha in the 1992 special issue of the DEC tech journal he wrote:

"The Alpha AXP architecture has no byte load or store instructions and
no implicit unaligned accesses. There also are no partial-register
writes. The byte load/store instructions and unaligned accesses found
in some RISC architectures can be a performance bottleneck. They require
an extra byte shifter in the speed-critical load and store paths, and
they force a hard choice in fast cache design".

Perhaps his cache comment in the last sentence was a veiled reference
to the abandoned idea for an ECC protected write back data cache.

--
