"Kurt Shoens" <shoens@lenny.sfrn.dnai.com> wrote in message
news:9k5ehi$25h$1@bob.news.rcn.net...
> In article <9k20nd$h97$1@pyrite.mv.net>, Bill Todd <billtodd@foo.mv.com>
wrote:
> >e.g., the volume of lock-related meta-data can threaten to exceed a
32-bit
> >address space in large installations.
>
> Eeek.  That's a lot of locks.  I'm sure the effort to avoid getting that
> many locks would be amply repaid.

Not necessarily:  in many cases it's possible to combine lock requests with
data requests and then cache the lock as long as the data is also cached,
thus largely eliminating the marginal expense of fine-granularity locking
(which of course can provide better concurrency - and if you support lock
de-escalation as IIRC DEC's Rdb does, they you need to maintain the
fine-grain context anyway just in case).  I tend to associate you with the
large pile of lock-related patents IBM owns, but realize that many of them
may have been from wholly separate research projects:  if you weren't
involved in this particular one, some of your colleagues were.

>
> Also, the notion of splitting locks over multiple address spaces or
> of using PAE to get > 32-bit buffer pool addressability is probably
> a poor tradeoff.  The database systems I've known are quite sensitive
> to the performance of getting to an already-buffered page and to
> acquiring/releasing locks.  It's a big jump to throw a system call into
> those costs ...

For lock operations, I agree (that's why I offered them up as an example of
64-bit utility).  For buffer access (as long as the buffer meta-data
structures are virtually resident) it's not so clear:  system calls don't
cost what they used to as long as the client process can retain its address
mapping, and database access to a buffer tends to have a non-negligible
operation path length such that adding modestly to it may not be noticeable
(especially considering that really frequently-accessed material may be
cached at a higher level than the disk buffer pool:  even with an
already-mapped buffer, first finding it and then finding some piece of data
within it has a fair amount of overhead).

- bill





