I agree ....

anyone who's ever done a really big timing-on-the-edge chip knows that 
using tools (such as an economy, or a gate compiler like Synopsis) that 
work with extremely large systems with exponetially large state spaces 
(such as an economy or a large block or gates say 50-200k) and just 
optimise for local minima is extremely unlikely to find the global minimum 
(like minimum gates, perfect timing or the most efficient economic system) 
they're searching for.

If you've done that reall hard chip and slogged away trying to make timing 
only to get a new release of the sythesis tool and find your 
almost-made-timing vanish in a cloud of smoke - you know you need to have 
some sort global optimisation (such as an engineer restructuring code, or a 
govt managing the economy in the large [think Greenspan]) to guide your 
optimisation tool to find that right global minimum.

(to be fair synopsys these days seems less sensitive to initial conditions 
than it used to - in part I suspect due to it doing more global sorts of 
optimisation up front).

Of course an economy isn't a chip (more like a hot plate than simulated 
anealing - though right now it looks like a bit of that's going on :-). 
But the more I think about the almost religious belief I keep seeing in the 
Freidman-esque school of thought that sais at it simplest that "personal 
greed causes an economy to run at its most efficient" - I know from my 
personal experience of optimising real-life large systems that it's a very 
simplistic view of the world (remember they give Nobels for economics, but 
not for mathematics - at least you'd think they'd get a few complexity 
theorists in to check his work, and give him a few pointers :-)

        Paul

PS: OB-vms/OB-unix - I did once port Unix (V6/V7) to run with the kernel in 
sup. mode in place of DCL .... but that was a long time ago ....


