mulp <michaelpettengill@earthlink.net> wrote:
> "Jordan Henderson" <jordan@lisa.gemair.com> wrote in message
> news:9ibj2h$9nt$1@lisa.gemair.com...
> >   What really drove the move from IA16 to IA32 was not customer
> >   demand for faster systems, but rather customer demand for
> >   the new system software (Windows386, OS/2, Unix) that required
> >   IA32.  This is what will drive the move from IA32 to IA64.

> What software exactly was it that drove the demand for 386?

For the people I knew who made the jump early, it was ports of Mac software
that really needed large address spaces a-la the 68000 to run with any kind
of decent performance. The first Windows versions on the 386 didn't give you
any way to do that, but when Win32s came out, so you could run 32-bit software
even on some older versions of Windows on the 386, it really took off.

The bottom line for most of these was simply that you couldn't store much of
a bitmap in 64k of memory.

In the 64-bit arena, it's large sparse address spaces for high-end databases.

I don't think there's yet that much demand for that kind of virtual memory in
end-user systems. Really good VR will probably need >32bit address space, too,
but that's some distance off (well, > 5 years).

