In comp.arch mulp <michaelpettengill@earthlink.net> wrote:

> "Jordan Henderson" <jordan@lisa.gemair.com> wrote in message
> news:9ibj2h$9nt$1@lisa.gemair.com...
>>   What really drove the move from IA16 to IA32 was not customer
>>   demand for faster systems, but rather customer demand for
>>   the new system software (Windows386, OS/2, Unix) that required
>>   IA32.  This is what will drive the move from IA32 to IA64.

> What software exactly was it that drove the demand for 386?

Most software that did drive it was 16 bit. It was well into the 486
times that 32 bit software became semi-important. Of course, ports of
various OS-s became a lot easier to x86 machines by having a new
(and one might say more sensible) VM usage model available.

> Windows 3.0 only used the memory management of the 386 to provide access to
> more memory to run 16 bit code; this was just a cheaper solution than some
> of the earlier memory expansion kludges that had been available previously.

Right. Also, unix ports became way easier. But what really mattered was that
esp. on the machines where there was on-motherboard cache, old software ran
a lot (to hughesly) faster than on a 286.

