wrote:
> > >In the 64-bit arena, it's large sparse address spaces for high-end
databases.
>
> > Please explain why this helps high-end databases.  The direct value
> > I can think of is the ability to address a large buffer pool that still
> > fits in physical memory.  Sparse addressing isn't necessary for that.
>
> I don't know any of the details...

One application is to in-memory meta-data structures that may frequently
need to be touched (hence could noticeably impede performance if placed in
'extended physical memory' that needed to be explicitly mapped before use) -
e.g., the volume of lock-related meta-data can threaten to exceed a 32-bit
address space in large installations.  There are, as always, ways to
partition the problem among multiple 32-bit address spaces to avoid such
issues, but since they add complexity to already-complex code the larger
address space has some value.

- bill





