news:slrn9m93gf.64f.glc+spam@adams.patriot.net...
> On 29 Jul 2001 16:38:08 GMT, Peter da Silva <peter@abbnm.com> wrote:
> [snip]
> >
> >In the 64-bit arena, it's large sparse address spaces for high-end
databases.
> >
>
> I'd go so far as to generalize this to dense address spaces as well. It's
> handy to use mmap() to pull in data.

Handy, possibly.  Inefficient, usually, since mmap doesn't know the size of
the chunk of data you need and unless the system's page-fetch algorithm just
happens to correspond with that size you'll either perform multiple fetches
from disk for a single chunk or fetch more than you need.  And when it comes
time to write the data, you almost always need fine control over when, and
how much, hits the disk.

Such use of mmap is a Unixism, and Unix has typically lagged a decade or
three behind when it comes to high-performance disk access (extent-based
file systems, which date at least back to the early 1970s and likely far
earlier, are still the exception rather than the rule).  I doubt that
high-end databases can throw away performance to that degree and remain
competitive.  Low-end databases may be able to, but then low-end databases
far less frequently need the larger address space.

- bill





