> >high-end databases can throw away performance to that degree and remain
> >competitive.  Low-end databases may be able to, but then low-end
databases
> >far less frequently need the larger address space.
>
> I use MapViewOfFile() as a drop-in replacement for mmap() under w2k.
> Does pretty well.

The question is, pretty well at what?  There are certainly applications that
can use this effectively (as well as applications where it's a really bad
idea), but typically databases (the area that was being discussed) have a
pretty well-defined means of accessing data:  it resides in structured pages
of one or a few fixed sizes, these pages are often accessed randomly, and
when written back to the database they're written in a tightly-controlled
manner (and usually in page units) w.r.t. log file writes.  Mapped access as
typically implemented in Unix (and in Windows) doesn't meet these
requirements well; neither do 'single-level store' approaches such as those
used in System/38 and its descendants unless the normal access methods are
supplemented with mechanisms to control such activity more finely.

- bill





