Bill Todd wrote:
> The question is, pretty well at what?  There are certainly applications that
> can use this effectively (as well as applications where it's a really bad
> idea), but typically databases (the area that was being discussed) have a
> pretty well-defined means of accessing data:  it resides in structured pages
> of one or a few fixed sizes, these pages are often accessed randomly, and
> when written back to the database they're written in a tightly-controlled
> manner (and usually in page units) w.r.t. log file writes.

man msync

You can tightly control your write-back times, at least within sync
points ("must be written before"). With log-structured writes, you
typically first announce updates into the log, then write the log, and
then update the in-core database pages; before starting the next log,
sync the updated pages.

If you use mmap without any sort of madvice or automatic prefetch, you
better stick to the CPU's page size for your data base. And current
pages are either too small or too large (4k vs. 4M).

