> shareholder suits if it cannot show that it is trying to generate
> profits.

I believe that in the US of A, corporations are generally required by
law to operate in the best interests of their stakeholders.  (This came
up at my employer a couple years ago during a corporate merge and
spin-off.)  The primary stakeholders are the owners (i.e.,
shareholders).  Other stakeholders include (in no particular order) the
company's creditors, neighbors, employees, directors, customers, and
anybody else affected by the company.

"In the best interests of" doesn't necessarily mean making a profit. 
For example, the owners of companies that don't pay dividends are
usually primarily interested in the stock price.

If course, if any directors don't meet the owners' expectations, they
can be replaced (sometimes certain owners have guaranteed seats on the
board).  If any directors (or the company) defraud (or otherwise cheat)
the stakeholders, of course, they may be subject to civil or criminal
suits.

Not a lawyer,
--Kevin
