> I was under the impression that Microsoft didn't offer dividends to
> shareholders. Is that correct ? If a company never offers dividends to
> shareholders, is that tantamount to a company not being profitable ?

Profitability and dividends are only very loosely linked.
Some very profitable companies don't pay dividends.  Some
companies pay dividends during years or quarters when
they have losses.

High Tech companies frequently pay no or very tiny
dividends on the theory that they can use the money
better than the shareholder can and because money
that goes to dividends is taxed twice - once on company
profit, once as shareholder income - whereas money
kept to grow (hopefully) the business is taxed only
once.

