How much does it save? Good question, perhaps Compaq has a Better Answer.

If we go with the EEtimes figure of $150M for Alpha development (as good a
number as any) and then factor in the cost of ancillary activities
(Alpha-related compilers, system development, support, etc) the $250M per
year guesstimate remains valid.

Deporting 100-150 chip architects and compiler writers to the Intel Gulag
won't save anywhere near $250M a year, and EV7/Marvel remains a high-profile
expense item. As Compaq goes forward, the savings should be appreciable.
Standardizing on a common CPU architecture will enable more ISSG and HPSD
joint development efforts, especially on post-Marvel and post-QuickBlade
stuff.

I wouldn't be surprised to see some sort of ISSG/HPSD consolidation, either.





