Peter Hancock wrote:
> 1. Nobody asked for VM. It was some kind of moonlight project
>    by a scientific computing group at IBM. 

I had heard it had been developped by some university or other educational
organisation and then adopted big time by IBM. (this would be similar to a
little known 370 operating system called MUSIC that had been developped by
McGill University in Montreal and resold by IBM - 150 users on an 8 meg of ram
IBM 370 back in early 1980s).

> 2. One of the main reasons that customers liked VM was that they
>    could test-run new or trial versions of buggy systems alongside the
>    current versions.

VM was also the basis for introducing interactive computing to DOS/VSE shops
which had previously been confined to batch jobs. The editor (was it EDGAR ?)
was actually quite OK.


> Is it possible to say in a sentence or two, or point to a short
> high-level description of what kind of communication interface there
> is/was between the virtual machines?  How do they look to each other?

Virtual card readers and punchers, as well as print spoolers allowed stuff to
flow from one virtual machine to another. That is how, for instance, MUSIC
under VM was able to submit jobs to an MVS system under VM and get the
listings back to MUSIC.

However, with VM, I beleive there was also the concept of shared disk drives
between interactive users. (you'd mount the virtual disk drive that contained
the source code of the project you're working on for instance, and so would
other co-workers so each of their instance of an OS (single user VM instance)
would map to the same files. However, I *THINK* that to send from VM to MVS or
DOS/VSE, it had to be either punched card format (80 byte records) or printed
listing format (133 byte records).

I know that when I worked for a large IBM shop back in early 1990s, to
transfer files from one host to another via their SNA network, they would run
a bacth job which would do the equivalent of MIME encoding of the dataset,
preeceded by some JCL and spooled to the remote host's batch queue quere, when
it ran, the program would read from its SYS$INPUT ( //SYSIN DD * ) and
reconstruct the dataset in its original format.


