Going back to my old documents has shown that my post had a couple errors.

First, Mulp was correct that Alpha did not originally have byte/word *stores*
in order to make ECC checking easier on level 1 caches.  However, Paul Demone
was also correct when he said that Alpha did not originally have byte/word
*loads* in order to make the MUX in the cache read path simpler.  I remembered
the arguments concerning *stores* because. as the representative from the
compiler group, I argued strongly in favor of 32-bit stores so that the
multiprocessor Alpha systems would support 32-bit memory granularity when
executing parallel Fortran programs with single-precision floating arrays.
When the hardware representatives backed down on 32-bit stores they also
gave us 32-bit loads as a bonus.

Second, a check of my EV-6 Chip Specification gives some details on the ECC
corrections in the EV-6 level 1 cache.  My chip specification gives some
interesting restrictions that were necessary to support ECC checking.
However, my chip specification is an early, preliminary version and I
know that some of its descriptions are wrong.  But, EV-6 does include
a write-back level 1 cache with some interesting ECC checking behind it.
Perhaps a more hardware oriented person, with an up-to-date specification,
could describe the implementation details.

Sorry for the inaccuracies in my original posting.



