In article <oqae1uycow.fsf_-_@premise.demon.co.uk>,
Peter Hancock  <pgh@dcs.ed.ac.uk> wrote:
>
>Is it possible to say in a sentence or two, or point to a short
>high-level description of what kind of communication interface there
>is/was between the virtual machines?  How do they look to each other?

There are several available mechanisms, in two classes.  In either case,
two virtual machines look to each other like separate real machines, with
various communication channels of varying capabilities.

Class 1:  Virtualised real I/O interfaces.  These are available to
          any operating system -- the OS need not even know that it
          is running in a virtual machine.

   Coupling-Facility channels:  very high performance, but I think this is
                                a restricted interface

   Channel-to-Channel adapter:  uses channel programs to read-write blocks;
                                high throughput for large-enough block sizes

   Shared disk:  if hypervisor implements minidisk caching, performance should
                 approach that of CTCA; otherwise disk limited

   Card reader/punch:  simple CCW interface but low performance

Class 2:  hypervisor-defined interfaces.  These are still machine-like
          in the way they are used (via privileged machine instructions
          and interrupt reflection, or direct storage access), but they
          go beyond what is available in a real machine.  The operating
          system has to be aware of the VM hypervisor (CP, the "Control
          Program").

   Shared "real" memory.  By mutual agreement two (or more) virtual machines
   can have a region of their real address space shared.  (The mapping that
   is performed by the hypervisor underneath is invisible.)  There is (or
   used to be) a mini-OS called GCS that exploits this.  There is even an
   architectural mode called XC (no real-machine equivalent) that extends
   S/390 Access Register mode to DAT-off operating systems like CMS to
   address multiple "real" address spaces, so one virtual machine's entire
   primary address space can (by mutual agreement, via hypervisor services)
   by accessible as another virtual machine's AR-selected data space.

   VMCF - Virtual Machine Communication Facility:  provides a DMA-like
   interface for sending and receiving packets.  Used for example to
   communicate with the VM-TCPIP server virtual machine.

   IUCV - Inter-User-Communication-Vehicle:  a more elaborate messaging
   facility with multiple channels, revocation mechanisms, etc.  Used by
   the CMS Shared Filesystem to communicate with the SFS server VM.

   LDF - Logical Device Facility:  allows one VM to act like a display
   control unit for another's virtual 3270 console.  Used by PVM -- the
   cross-network VM logon mechanism.

   High-performace access to the virtual card reader (the primary "network"
   interface between virtual machines, where one VM's Punch feeds into
   another VM's Reader), allowing 4K blocks of virtual cards to be read in
   one gulp.

Historically, the coupling between virtual card punches and readers (across
the RSCS company-wide network, aka VNET) was the primary communication
interface (nearly 30 years old).  It may sound quaint, but it's really just
a data pipe (with 80-byte blocks) with tagged ends to perform the connection,
and it has a really simple interface (no connection protocols etc. -- the
network is store-and-forward, so it's literally like mailing postcards where
bunches (individual "spool files") are guaranteed to arrive in order (there
is no virtual equivalent to a deck of cards spilled on the floor).

Michel





