Yesterday I received a "Sun Offers" brochure from Sun UK addressed to
"A Greig, VMS Systems Manager"

Today several of us, including the Europe/Eastern Hemisphere IT
Director, received a postcard from Sun which says:

DUMPED?
Alpha recovery campaign from Sun

in big bold letters on the front. The other side carries a quote from
CW360 saying:
"Compaq stunned the IT world this week by selling its 64 bit Alpha
chip technology to Intel and pledging to move all its server offerings
to Intel's Itanium chip by 2004. It is a massive leap of faith,
because the low-cost IA64 architecture was late, is unproven and still
lacks Windows support.

It should encourage users of all solutions based on this to look for
better deals from server giants in the next few years. Particaularly
from Sun, whose business strategy, while also towards services, is
based on the magic properties of the RISC/UNIXtm combination"
CSW360 Friday 29 June 2001
www.cw360.com

It then goes on to offer a 20% trade in value on any new Sun server
against an Alphaserver.

So far I have not yet received any *formal* written communication from
Compaq but Sun have managed to get out two. Compaq appear to be
massively incompetent. Possibly the Alpha group has no expletive
budget to send out reassuring glossies to users.

You can see the online version at http://sun.co.uk/alpha

Like the animation:
"Dumped? Lost, Lonely, Stabbed in the Back?
Therapy from Sun"

When will Compaq fight back or are they repeating the DEC mistake of
pushing 70% of the remaining customer base towards Sun or HP?

Here's my current take  on the matter. It doesn't matter if there were
possible future difficulties ahead for Compaq in keeping up Alpha
performance vs IA64, Even if there were they had to at least make the
effort. Signaling you will give up at the very first sign you might
need to spend money sends all the wrong signs and only Wintel centred
management could possibly not see this. My gut feeling is still  that
Compaq have signed their own death warrant., that Tru64 will
eventually be submerged by Linux (with Compaq's help) and that
bizarrely only VMS might just make it through - maybe owned by someone
else. I used to get laughed at for suggesting that VMS would outlive
Alpha/NT...
--
Alan


