It seems to be time to tie together a few issues, and Robert's post below
touches on some of them.

I'll start with a disturbing email I received yesterday, with the subject
line "You were right about Alpha".  It came from someone who had been fairly
vigorously (and politely) suggesting (as Robert does below) that things like
private statements from Alpha engineers and arm's-length analysis of (mostly
public) financial information weren't anything like a reasonable basis for
assessing Compaq's statements about Alpha's future viability.

Specific earlier suggestions from this individual had included that we
really didn't have any indication that EV8 hadn't run into serious technical
or schedule problems of one kind or another and/or that Alpha wouldn't have
had difficulty retaining a significant performance lead over IA64.

Indeed, several people have advanced the thesis that because our information
on such matters is far from perfect we really aren't justified in concluding
*anything*.  Perhaps they were unduly influenced by the study of Newtonian
mechanics at an early age, but I suspect that it may say more about their
reluctance to believe ill of their vendor:   there's something just a tad
disingenuous about refusing to accept tangible (even if incomplete) evidence
on one side of an issue simply because it does not also include proof that
evidence on the other side of that issue could not exist.

To return to the email, it indicated that the author had just been contacted
privately by "someone I trust deeply on knowing Alpha internals as well as
R/D futures" and was now happy to "concede that there probably isn't an
internal technical gap inside Alpha engineering w.r.t. to feasibility (as
Dennis vaguely hinted at in comp.arch)."

The disturbing part of the email was that it began with this statement:
"I'm keeping this private to avoid further speculation in c.o.v."  The
author, who had been himself vigorously speculating in comp.os.vms in
defense of Compaq's position when he had no real information on the matter,
was suddenly reluctant to say anything now that he actually had something of
substance (as least in his opinion) to contribute.

I pointed this out to him, and he responded that his interest in the matter
had been to try to provide balance rather than to take any active side on
the issue (getting involved in such things not being his cup of tea).  I
accept that explanation and certainly respect his wish to remain anonymous,
but since he did not request confidentiality for the information provided
(nor do his reasons for not getting involved himself imply such a desire) it
seems reasonable to release it - since I believe it's significant in
multiple respects and I'd like others to have the opportunity to draw their
own conclusions about it.

Moving onward:

"Robert Deininger" <rdeininger@mindspring.com> wrote in message
news:rdeininger-2207011410280001@user-2ive7ua.dialup.mindspring.com...

...

> I've followed most of your discussions.  I haven't seen anything blatantly
> wrong with your numbers.  However, I think your interpretation is on
> thinner ice.

The question is, thinner than what?  It certainly appears to be considerably
thicker than the ice that Compaq's assertions are standing on.

>
> I don't recall anyone from Compaq saying that Alpha was a money pit.

My response was to someone who did (that's why I quoted him).  And while
that particular phrase may not have been used by any Compaq official, it's
certainly the impression they've been attempting to convey (as you state in
your next sentence) via those people (particularly Terry) who have been
fronting for them.

...

> We have seen hints, if not outright statements from Compaq, that the cost
> of funding future alpha development was a factor in the cancellation.
> Your analysis of past expenditures really doesn't, and can't, address what
> they would have had to spend to complete EV8 as it should have been
> completed.  Maybe there were significant problems on the horizon that we
> haven't heard about.

And maybe an asteroid will strike the Alpha engineering team tomorrow, in
which case future funding would of course turn out to have been
questionable.

Aside from the points I made (and the additional input I related) above, we
have the following to guide us:

1.  Alpha has a 10-year development history already, and a long-term
development plan that has emphasized a staged implementation in an orderly
manner (i.e., without assumptions of the form "here a miracle occurs").  The
road ahead was clearly delineated and no evidence (not even hearsay) of a
major detour has been offered.

2.  The 1999 annual report (page 42) projected future R&D expenditures for
yet-unrealized technology out through 2005 at about $500 million (per year
for all development; another later comment suggests that the largest single
contributor would have been storage, with Alpha a solid second) annually
through 2003 and then decreasing.  While schedule slips could have pushed
out the 'knee' in this curve, it suggests that planned funding was at least
fairly constant at that time.

3.  The 2000 annual report indicates (on page 17) that the development-cost
estimates had been reduced by about $100 million per year (compared with the
1999 estimates) for the years 2001 - 2004.  This reflects either even
greater confidence in the ability to continue to develop Alpha
cost-effectively or that killing Alpha was already contemplated when that
report was issued.

  If Compaq could not or would not commit to spending
> enought money to ensure success in the face of difficult technical
> problems, it's quite possible that the more technical folks would start to
> seriously consider alternatives.

If you can't produce even a hint of evidence of such 'difficult technical
problems' (beyond the admitted inherent difficulty of leading microprocessor
development, but that's been factored in all along), the preceding sentence
is nothing but hot air.

And here is as good a place as any to confront Dennis' contention that the
VMS revenue/profit numbers I related from a year ago in defense of a
reasonable level of Alpha development expenditure were fabricated (a
contention as vacuous as the rest of his posts on the subject).  While I
have other sources for some of that information, the 'unimpeachable' source
is known to both Terry Shannon and Rob Young, both of whom can hardly be
considered to be on my side of this issue.  If one of them would like to
offer arguments against this source's credibility, I'll be happy to name him
and allow people to make their own judgement:  the information was not
accompanied by a request for anonymity, but other things being equal I'm
inclined to allow people to identify themselves if they choose to rather
than take it upon myself to do so.

Thanks to 'billmuy' for providing the pointer to the 2000 annual report that
doesn't seem to be available on Compaq's Web site.  It confirms the
conclusions I drew from the quarterly figures I reported previously:  the
Enterprise Computing segment lead in both percentage and total profitability
in 2000, even after the services portion was split out, while the
business/consumer PC side of the house contributed less than 11% to gross
income even though it accounted for about 50% of revenue.  In other words,
the Enterprise and services areas were over 8 times as profitable - in both
absolute and percentage terms - as the business/consumer PC business in 2000
(before the latter dropped into the red in 2001Q1 and made the ratio
meaningless).

Pages 11 - 13 make interesting reading.  They indicate that Enterprise
Computing's 10% revenue bump over 1999 was almost completely due to
increased sales of industry-standard servers (at least when storage revenue
is ignored), but are much more coy about attributing credit for the nearly
80% profit increase in the same time frame, saying only that "Margins
improved due to a mix shift toward the high-end and increased enterprise
storage."

Combine this with the statement that "Strong growth in enterprise storage,
which consisted of external storage, software and high-end tape, was offset
by lower attached storage" and you have at least the suggestion that, while
'industry-standard' servers may have accounted for more than half the
revenue (and all the revenue growth) when storage was *not* included, Alpha
and Tandem systems (especially if you include their associated storage) were
generating most of the profit (which supported selling industry-standard
servers at near-cost in order to hold or gain market share - gee, where have
we seen *that* before?).

Add in the statement that Alpha sales suffered due to
GS-introduction-related component shortages and this hardly constitutes an
indictment against Alpha's current and future profitability (rather the
reverse) - and the numbers remain consistent with the conclusions I drew
previously from the 1999 report.

I realize that many people may not particularly enjoy plowing through
financial statements and extracting information from them - I certainly
don't.  But people who aren't willing to do so really don't have much to
base their opinions on in such matters compared with those of us who have
made that effort - and they shouldn't expect too much respect for such
comparatively unfounded opinions.

  If your study of past financials allows
> you to predict what alpha's future costs would have been with any
> certainty, well, I guess I missed that post in the recent blizzard.

Certainty in most things is unattainable, so people in the real world tend
to operate on the best information available (which is what I've tried to
present).  If Compaq or its apologists have released *any* information
supporting their assertions on these questions, I haven't seen it:  it's all
been speculation similar to what you've offered above (and you even state so
explicitly in the following sentence).

>
> I don't have any facts to support this kind of scenario.  I'm just
> pointing out that your analyses (which have started to get rather shrill,
> btw) do not exhaust all the possibilities.  You seem to want to put the
> worst possible face on Compaq's actions _and_ motives.  That's fine.  You
> have your axe, feel free to grind it.  But perhaps you should re-evaluate
> your supposed monopoly on the Truth before you shout "bullspit" at those
> you disagree with.

I think 'bullshit' is an entirely appropriate characterization for
statements made persistently and without accompanying evidence of any kind
that purport to refute other assertions that have been at least moderately
well-documented.  In fact, that comes close to a definition of the idiom.

And I don't apologize for becoming annoyed at times with people seemingly
(and again persistently) incapable of understanding this difference between
substantiated argument and demagoguery.

- bill

