Hi Bill,

Interesting comments you made about Compaq financials.  They can
sometimes be a goldmine of information. You are quite right about the PC
market (personal and commercial) being a consistent money loser and
dragging Compaq down.

A couple of brief comments:

- In a GOOD quarter PC's seem to return a profit about 1 or 2% of the
money spent on them; in most quarters they lose money and have done for
the last few years.  By contrast Dell are returning about 6-10%. 
Enterprise stuff from Compaq regularly returns about 14%.

- In the Annual Report for 2000 (ie. December) Compaq says about
Commercial Personal Computing, "Costs for processors, memory and hard
drives for desktops and portables declined during the year.", and about
Consumer PC's "Higher component costs also contributed to lower
operating income."  The contradiction is not impossible but it does
deserve an explanation.

- The April 2001 figures are interesting because Commercial PC's and
Consumer PC's are now under the same umbrella, called "Access".  The
report says  "The following restated prior period data is provided for
informational purposes as a result of the segment reporting changes made
in the first quarter of 2001".  (ie. they are posting the last five
quarters of data according to this new classification.)  Summing the 4
quarters of 2000 and comparing it to the stated Annual figures, the
interesting thing is that the Revenue for "Access" is simply an addition
of the old 2 categories but somehow the income to all of the
classifications has been shuffled around.  In particular the income from
"Enterprise Computing" is down by $400 million (revenue unchanged) and
income from Global Services has risen from $884 million to $944 million
and revenue has changed to $6993 million from $7483 million. 
Interestingly revenue for all PC products (now called "Access") is
unchanged but the income is up from $243 million to $459 million.

Most importantly the TOTAL INCOME for the four quarters is now $2803
million compared to $3570 million shown in the annual statement.  What
has happened to the other $750 million - more than 20% of the profit
that was stated in the annual report ?  If anyone sees it, please let me
know.  (If anything is seriously wrong with my calculations please also
let me know !)

-  For anyone who thinks that shareholders might be worried about Compaq
it is a realtively easy business to use www.yahoo.com or www.nasdaq.com
to discover who the institutional shareholders are.  Using the Nasdaq
site you can click on their names to see what other holdings these
people have.  I suspect that Barclays (the largest) is like many of the
others.  Barclays have holdings in IBM, Sun, Dell, HP, Cisco, Oracle
....etc.  Realistically they probably don't care much about which IT
company wins or loses because they are "betting" on all the players.


Okay, enough of these interesting snippets and back to the matter at
hand.  Yes, the decision to move to Alpha appears to have been primarily
motivated by the bean-counters.

But it is time for a non-aggressive, "So what ?".

The decision has been made by Compaq - for better or for worse - and we
just have to try to make the best of it.   Can they back out of the deal
?  Probably only with extreme difficulty.  Do they want to back out of
it ?  Probably very unlikely.

What we need now to move forward are some reassuring statements from
Compaq and some commitments about how they plan to handle the
transition.  It is not just this newsgroup that wants and/or needs these
statements, there are a lot of VMS sites who would like to know what is
going on.

It is now almost a month since the big statement from Compaq, a
statement with little detail and absolutely no follow-up.  I really did
expect something better from them.

 

John McLean



