JF Mezei wrote:
> 
> Peter Hancock wrote:
> > I though VM was the underlying thing, and MVS was one of the things
> > VM could host?
> 
> This was the "old way".  But MVS-only shops can run multiple instances of MVS
> on a 390 or higher without the presence of VM. This saves a lot of overhead
> since IO is not intercepted by VM.

What you're talking about is LPAR - Logical PARtitioning. It's a
closer-to-the-hardware way of splitting of a given machine into
smaller ones. Two levels of sort-of-virtualization: LPAR, inside
of which you can run MVS or VM or (others less known). Then
inside that VM system you can run many more multiple systems than
LPAR can do: thousands, vs. a max of 256 (I think that's the LPAR
limit, anyway).

> I did hear that VMS was making a comeback to run multiple instances of Linux
> on IBM mainframes though.

I think you meant VM, right? In that case, sort of. VM is much
more talked about in these latter days of hosting 100s of Linux
machines on a single LPAR, but it never did fade away. It's
always been used, at least to debug new releases of MVS if not
for other reasons.

Greg Pfister


