Since there are people here who know a lot about these
things, can I take the opportunity to check a couple of possible
myths? 

1. Nobody asked for VM. It was some kind of moonlight project
   by a scientific computing group at IBM.  However IBM were
   smart enough to realise what they had. 

2. One of the main reasons that customers liked VM was that they
   could test-run new or trial versions of buggy systems alongside the 
   current versions.  So the attraction was in large part one of dealing
   with software faults. 

Is it possible to say in a sentence or two, or point to a short
high-level description of what kind of communication interface there
is/was between the virtual machines?  How do they look to each other?

Peter Hancock


