In article <oqae1uycow.fsf_-_@premise.demon.co.uk>, Peter Hancock <peter@premise.demon.co.uk> writes:
:Since there are people here who know a lot about these
:things, can I take the opportunity to check a couple of possible
:myths? 

  You have made a central myth-stake :-), as you are asking IBM VM 
  questions in the Compaq OpenVMS (VMS) operating system newsgroup.

:Is it possible to say in a sentence or two, or point to a short
:high-level description of what kind of communication interface there
:is/was between the virtual machines?  How do they look to each other?

  The typical virtual machine implementation provides virtualized I/O
  devices, meaning that guest operating systems -- potentially including 
  a virtual machine itself operating as a guest of another virtual
  machine -- all see themselves as running on what appears to be the
  hardware.  

  One of the key to good virtual machine performance is intercepting
  as few instructions and as few I/O calls as is possible, as the more 
  intercepts and the more emulation that is required, the slower the 
  overall operation.

  I would encourage most any operating system text book for details
  on virtual memory and virtualizing an operating system.

  As for discussions of any IBM VM mythos and/or pathos (if any, of 
  course), please visit an IBM-related newsgroup.

