> our xp1000 refuses to boot anymore, during bootup we get the message:
> 
> ------
> panic (cpu0): ftx_recovery_pars: log read error (1)
> N1= -1065
> syncing disks ... done
> 
> DUMP: No primary swap; no explicit dumpdev, nowhere to put header 
> giving up.
> ------
> 
> shortly after that the machine reboots again.
> 
> We can boot via CD, disklabel shows no obvios problems, swap partitions
> are still there (at least they seem to...).
> 
> any hints are very much appreciatet....

I wonder if your crash really has anything to do with a swap problem.  A 
lot of operating systems, when they crash, will do a memory dump to the 
swap partition, for debugging purposes.  If you don't have a primary 
swap partition then you might get an error.  The primary swap partition 
is specified by making a link /sbin/swapdefault that points to the swap 
partition's entry in the /dev directory.  Check to make sure that 
/sbin/swapdefault exists and points to a valid swap partition.

As for "log error" I think this is a problem with an AdvFS journal log.  
That's certainly a "log" error that might cause a panic during boot.  If 
you do man advfs_err you will see that an advfs error code -1065 
indicates there was an error access the file domain transaction log.  

Maybe you have a heavily corrupted filesystem or bad blocks on your 
disk.  When you boot from the CD, can you mount the drive's filesystems 
okay?  If they are AdvFS, do they check out with the /sbin/advfs/verify 
command?

