I quite agree that systems engineering is the important issue. But for that,
the processor is only a small piece of the puzzle, cf. the Origin 3000, which
certainly is a stellar system based on a so-so processor. Given that the
per-processor performance differential between a current Alpha and a current
x86 system is all but negligible, it would have been quite possible to design
and build systems of comparable qualitiy, reliability, scaleability etc. based
on a Pentium III or P4 instead of a 21264. See also the trade-offs of merchant
hardware vs everything homebuilt in the historical cases of KSR and Tera.

