That's the implication that I thought was too obvious to require explicit
expansion when I made the statement you quoted just above.

And it's only one consideration.  Another is that even a low IA64 cost won't
make any difference if the (Alpha) processor cost isn't a significant
fraction of the system costs in the markets Alpha needs to succeed in (which
likely include low-end configurations mostly as adjuncts to larger systems
or as development platforms).  In this context, one should also remember
that (especially with the amount of on-chip 'glue' present in EV7) most of
the ancillary components in such systems can leverage the same commodity
components that IA64 can where this is appropriate.

 Whether that goal is achievable
> is another matter.

Indeed it is.

So far, there's nothing (with the possible exception of dedicated
cryptographic work) that Merced can do with better absolute performance than
the available alternatives - let alone anywhere nearly as cost-effectively.
And while McKinley promises to be faster (though one should remember that
even after down-playing Merced's performance Intel promised higher clock
speeds right up to the point where it proved, well after the first systems
were running, that this just wasn't going to happen - and now it appears
that McKinley may have suffered its first clock-speed erosion as well)
there's no indication that McKinley, even if it meets its current targets,
will be noticeably faster than the then-current Alpha EV6 offerings, let
alone EV7 or the single-processor (let alone CMP) POWER 4.

And that's only its high-end competition.  At the low end, IA32 from Intel
itself and AMD will be far more cost-effective for *all* levels of 32-bit
application performance for at least several years, and unless Intel manages
to do to AMD-64 what it just did to Alpha there's a lot of reason to believe
that AMD's upward-compatible extension strategy (and streamlined development
costs compared with IA64's) will give it a major edge in cost-effectiveness
in the lower-end even where 64-bit processing is desirable.

But the root issue in this sub-thread was the question of Alpha's economic
viability.  Even if IA64 *would have* managed to ship in large volumes
(despite such competition), I don't see any insights that call into question
Alpha's potential to thrive (though likely without matching those large
volumes if they indeed occur).

