Kilgallen@eisner.decus.org.nospam (Larry Kilgallen) writes:

> >> Systems involving multiple components that operate asynchronously are 
> >> quite difficult.
> > But so is thoroughly testing them, right ?  But as I said, I don't know why
> > formal verification isn't more widely used.
> Formal verification won't detect a faulty specification.

Well, formal verification should really be part of formal design, which would
include the specification.

The one commercial example I know of was the design of the T800 FPU. Its first
step was translating the specification - IEEE 754 - into Z, a language suited
to the purpose. It found a number of points that were unclear and had to be
"clarified". The rest of the design went through a number of steps, almost all
in software (some specially written for the purpose), down to the level of the
silicon compiler.

The formal design team found several errors in existing implementations (i.e.,
(co-)processors implementing IEEE 754, for instance the 68020). AFAIR, there
was one bug found in the implementation years later in an obscure place, but I
can't remember the details.

	Jan


