Bernd Paysan wrote:

> Sander Vesik wrote:
> > That's interesting. I believe that most of patent infringements in the world
> > happen because either:
> >         * people didn't know somebody had already invented and patented it
>
> Didn't the US patent law had a clause that independent reinvention
> allows you to use your invention though?
>

No; the standard example that I used to use, to emphasize to my students the
importance of keeping an accurate lab notebook, was that "Edison invented the
light bulb" -- known to all because the patent court found in his favor:  he
could document the critical step (if I recall correctly, the second evacuation,
after briefly powering up the filament, to outgas adsorbed molecules from its
surface) five days before Hiram Percy Maxim (who also invented a machine gun) did
the same thing, independently.

The patent system exists to reward those inventors who advance other people's
knowledge by disclosing their invention, rather than keeping it as a trade
secret.

