The estimate die size for 21264B and for AthlonC is about 120mm2 and the
estimate for P4 is 220mm2.  I can't find a current estimate for either
Merced or McKinley; and old one was 300-400 for Merced but that was when it
was going to be shipping in 1998, but since it includes a Pentium core plus
all sort of other stuff, I'll guess 300.

Assuming a uniform random distrtibution of defects and similar defect rates
for all similar processes, the defects would be about 1:4:9 because rate
goes up with the square of the area.

EV7 is going to be a huge chip with an estimated size of 400, but for much
of the die, there is considerable ECC protection and redundant memory arrays
that can be trimmed out.  Also, EV7 is glueless so there are not additional
chips required other than a IO-PCI interface chip to build a multiprocessor.
The chip cost will be higher, but the system cost will be lower than for a
multiprocessor P4 or Merced or McKinley system.





