"Sander Vesik" <sander@haldjas.folklore.ee> wrote ...
> In comp.arch Hamlyn Mootoo <univms@bigfoot.com> wrote:
> > I think you miss the purpose of a patent.  A patent rewards the FIRST
> > ONE who can devise or improve something with (presumably) the protection
> > to realize FINANCIAL gain from it.
>
> Wrong. US patents aren't anything like that.

Right. In fact, U.S. patents only give you the right to stop other
people from using your innovation.  They don't confer the
right for you to actually use it.  Sounds strange ? I'll explain:

Let's say Ed invented the vacuum-filled incandescent
electric lamp and patents it. Ed can now make vacuum
filled electric lamps (assuming no other patents interfere),
and forbid Bob from doing so.

Now, let's say Bob discovers that electric lamps
work better if filled with inert gas.  He patents
that, and can now prevent Ed from making gas-filled
incandescent electric lamps.  However, Ed's patents are
necessary to make _any_ kind of incandescent electric
lamp, so Bob can't make vacuum- *or* gas-filled
incandescent electric lamps.

Now, if Ed wants to make gas-filled lamps badly enough,
he'll go to Bob and negotiate for the rights to the gas-filled
lamp patent.  Maybe Ed will just pay Bob, or maybe Bob
and Ed will grant each other the rights to use each other's
patents (or some subset thereof): that latter is called
"cross-licensing".

So anyway, this shows that a patent doesn't give you
the right to do something, only the right to forbid others
to do it.
