Sander Vesik wrote:
> That's interesting. I believe that most of patent infringements in the world
> happen because either:
>         * people didn't know somebody had already invented and patented it

Didn't the US patent law had a clause that independent reinvention
allows you to use your invention though?

>         * somebody patented something they read in a magazine and the rest
>           don't know yet

That's probably the worst one for the patents we discuss here. You read
something in MPR, or in a conference paper, and you implement it, sell
chips, and after several years, this thing pops up in the USPTO data
base - patents routinely take 2-5 years between file and grant (now
that's a loooong pipeline ;-). Often, issued patents are already
obsolete, replaced by something better, but your competitor now has a
way to threat you.

> Wrong. US patents aren't anything like that.

Indeed. Single patents aren't worth anything. Patent portfolios are
build up by companies to bargain with other companies - cross patent
licensing is a sort of "piece pipe smoking in the big wigwam". These
patent portfolios are used mainly to prevent newcomers from entering a
market. Patents also do not describe true innovations, but small
engineering details, especially since it is too difficult to get true
innovations through the examiners - small details are possible, because
the examiner then is on firm ground. That's not just for US patents,
also Konrad Zuse's patent on the programmable computer was rejected by
the German PTO, because they could not see where the innovation was, and
all the parts they could understand (relays, papertape, etc.) were
already known.

At least that's the situation with hard- and software, other fields of
innovations may differ.

