There is very little difference between Windows CE on the Casio A-10,
my first WinCE-based handheld, and my Jornada 548... as far as the
software goes. Transcriber/calligrapher is still a second class
citizen. The menus are on the bottom ofthe display instead of the top.
The window decorations are 2d instead of 3d. There's a couple of
different applications. But the big difference is the Casio was a 40
MHz processor with a black-and-white screen and alkaline batteries,
whereas the Jornada is a 133 MHz processor with a color screen and
lithium-ion rechargables. Moore's Law, again, is what Microsoft depends
on to solve their problems.

> The same is bound to happen with NT. They will eventually have a version that
> is good enough to be trustable for enterprise applications. And only the
> strongest competitors will stay in the race with the smaller niche market ones
> dropping out.

The fundamental design of NT is actually pretty good. The problem is
that you don't really work on NT, you work on the Win32 subsystem. All
access to the NT kernel is mediated by an API that is relentlessly
single-instance. And much of the API is no longer in Microsoft's
control, but rather the application writers... people don't buy an OS
to run an OS, they buy it to run applications... and Microsoft can't
change behaviour that those applications depend on.

A Win32 program has hardcoded registry and file system locations, so
you can really only run a single instance of an application under it. A
Win32 program assumes it has write-access to the entire system, and
breaks if you attempt to secure it. A Win32 program assumes only a
single user instance is running on the machine, and the security model
breaks down if you have some trusted and some untrusted code running
concurrently.

I can imagine Microsoft producing an operating system suited for a server
environment, where multiple applications not designed to interoperate
run on a single computer, but it won't be anything like Windows NT as
we know it. This isn't something Moore's Law can solve for them.

