I suppose we can all stop pretending Dec is Compaq now.

The problem with many compilers is passing a view of the top level forest
down to the code generator. The Dec compilers are particularly good
at recognising access patterns to generate prefetching and write hinting.
In the real world timely cache management can be as or more important
scheduling 8 queens effectively.

Both the intel and HP compiler groups are very good too, but
Dec has had both a history of targetting more registers
than intel (as does HP), but has also been supporting x86
so retargetting may not be as big an issue for them as HP.
So personally, I wouldn't bet against the Dec people reapplying the
compiler technology *very* competitively, whether wholesale
or by integration of key parts and optimisation techniques.

