Your question-mark after "processor" above was appropriate:  the Alpha
*system* run rate is around 100K, but the processor run rate is 3 - 5 times
that (according to Terry's numbers), which is hardly surprising given that
the primary use of Alpha is in medium-to-large multi-processor systems.

Thus the per-processor development cost - even at the anemic sales figures
that its traditional stealth marketing generates - was in the $500 - $800
area.  This is indeed not all that much less than a bottom-end Athlon
(probably more cost-effective than a Pentium in this area) system sells for,
but that's been true for a while now and such systems don't seem to have
made much difference to Alpha's main market segments.

And, as I've observed elsewhere, at current run rates the IA64 per-processor
development burden likely approaches the GNP of many smaller countries.

The bottom line is that even with its current development-cost burden (which
could easily be halved or better with any reasonable marketing effort)
Alpha's per-processor cost is a negligible portion of the overall cost (not
even considering TCO) of the kinds of systems that make it profitable.  So
it's a non-issue until such systems are no longer needed in the marketplace
(i.e., until commodity high-volume *systems* offer the same degree of
quality, availability, and scalability - all of which have so far proved too
expensive to incorporate into such high-volume commodity products).

