Hamlyn Mootoo wrote:
> > > I think you miss the purpose of a patent.  A patent rewards the FIRST
> > > ONE who can devise or improve something with (presumably) the protection
> > > to realize FINANCIAL gain from it.
> >
> > Wrong. US patents aren't anything like that.
> 
> Care to elaborate, or maybe you didn't because you can't back up that
> statement. The following is an ACTUAL citation from Barrons Law
> Dictionary:
> PATENT OF INVENTION
> A grant of right to exclude others from making, using or selling of an
> invention during a specified time; it constitutes a ligitimate monoploy.
> See 304 F. Supp.357,367.
...

A couple years ago I read an article written by an attorney who
practiced patent law; I don't know whether that's all he did.  He said
the US patent system's original theoretical purposes were:

1.  Encouraging people to invent things, which is supposed to be good
for the nation.  They do this by increasing the chance that the
inventor's investment will be recouped.

2.  Encouraging people to manufacture newly invented things or use newly
invented processes, which is supposed to be good for the nation.  They
do this by increasing the chance that the manufacturer's investment will
be recouped.

3.  Encouraging people to publish their inventions in a public place, so
they become public knowledge instead of trade secrets.  This knowledge
is supposed to make it easier for later inventors and manufacturers and
discoverers, which is supposed to be good for the nation.

That's the theory, anyway.  In practice, of course, the system is
manipulated for the gain of the individuals and businesses involved.

He went on to claim that the Japanese and European systems have
different intentions, and that the American system was drifting away
from its base and toward those systems.  He claimed that this was a Bad
Thing because it benefitted large companies at the expense of individual
inventors.  That part of the article was clearly an opinion piece and
thus presumably slanted, but the first part sounded fairly
straightforward, but could have been slanted more than I realized.

--Kevin Strietzel
  Not speaking for Stratus.


