Now that you seem to have un-kill-filed me, would you care to explain your
flame Tuesday night?

Meanwhile, I'd be happy to elucidate (and I've expanded the distribution
back to its original scope for the occasion):

The Alpha annual development costs have been aired recently and often:
eetimes estimated them at $150 million (IIRC based on staffing), Winkler
provided a $300 million figure in a context that makes it unlikely to be at
all on the low side (especially considering that it's more than half of the
annual roughly $500 million projected for *all* DEC-related development in
the 1999 annual report, in which at the time storage was considered to be
the dominant feature), and you yourself have estimated them at $250 million.

An adequate basis for my assertion requires little additional information
beyond VMS's profitability as of a bit over a year ago.  As you're well
aware, Compaq is tight-lipped about exactly where its profits come from
(they don't even seem to have any real 2000 annual report available on the
Web, at least from a quick search), so this is the best fine-grained
evidence available and - again as you know - these figures come from an
unimpeachable source.  At that point in time, the annual VMS-related revenue
was about $4 billion (I've also heard $3.9 billion elsewhere) and the annual
VMS-related profit was about $800 million.

Since then, VMS revenues have grown modestly (according to your own
statements) and there's no indication that VMS profits haven't remained at
least similar to what they were (besides, if they had plummeted, Compaq
would have little reason to be making even its current feeble attempts to
retain that customer base).  Even if we assume that this enumeration of
VMS's profits takes the broadest possible view, includes *all* VMS system
revenue and service, and does *not* include Alpha development costs, these
VMS-related profits alone far more than cover *all* of Alpha development.

Now, you didn't say that the Alpha business had sub-optimal profitability
(though in the context of the profitability of the rest of Compaq's stable,
save for Tandem, that statement would have been easy to contest as well), or
that the Alpha business had *marginal* profitability (a statement even
easier to refute):  you said it was "economically unsustainable", a
statement so excessive that characterizing it as a "crock of shit" is richly
deserved even if we look no farther than VMS.

But I'm not done yet, because of course VMS is not the sole source of Alpha
profits.  And while I don't have the definite figures in other areas that I
had for VMS, I do have semi-quantitative data - often from you.

Tru64 large server sales reportedly (I don't remember the citation, but
since Tru64 suffers from merely inadequate rather than effectively
non-existent marketing this is hardly surprising) exceed VMS's, so the VMS
numbers above should (very) roughly double when *all* Alpha revenue and
profit is taken into account (plus at least something from the Alpha Linux
contingent).

And then there's the marketing issue itself - since "economically
unsustainable" clearly implies that Alpha was a dead duck *even if Compaq
had actually tried to make it fly*.

Unfortunately we must again go back a bit over a year to find a solid
fine-grained figure.  And that figure is a $13 million marketing budget for
VMS.  But there's another figure available in the 1999 annual report:  $385
million Compag advertising expenditures (seems suspiciously low to me -
perhaps because 'advertising' is only one aspect of 'marketing' - but I'll
use it).  Now, total Compaq revenues in 1999 were $38.5 billion, so
VMS-related revenue accounted for a tad over 10% of that.  And gross Compaq
income in 1999 was $8.7 billion, so VMS-related income (even if it was gross
income without adjustments for sales/administrative/R&D costs) accounted for
a tad under 10% of that.  And of course Compaq benefited from mutual
advertising underwritten by Microsoft and Intel, so had at least the
opportunity to shift at least a bit of that $385 million in the direction of
its more profitable products (hint:  not the Microsoft- and Intel-based
ones).

So it's clear that VMS was getting the decidedly short end of the marketing
stick (if that wasn't clear already) - leaving aside any suspicion that a
fair amount of that VMS marketing money went toward generic or shared Alpha
advertising (the little of that that there was), since it's really hard to
remember seeing any VMS-specific marketing beyond some bouncing, lighted
balls (maybe it just went to salaries...).

Since VMS has pretty regularly (your figures again, I believe) been
replacing something like 15% annual customer attrition with an equivalent
number of new customers *without* the benefit of noticeable marketing - or
convincing support of any kind - from Compaq, it's not much of a stretch to
imagine that with even modest encouragement a significantly larger number of
new customers could be attracted (hey, even if VMS accounts for half the
Alphas sold, it doesn't take that much to increase a mere 50,000 systems
sold annually by a significant amount).  And since Tru64 marketing has been,
shall we say, less than aggressive, one might expect a bounce there as well
with anything resembling visible 'commitment' (not the word, but the act)
from Compaq.

So let's add up the total so far:

1.  VMS's annual profit alone makes the assertion that the Alpha business
was "economically unsustainable" correctly characterizable as "a crock of
shit".  Q.E.D.

2.  The combination of VMS's and Tru64's annual profit (admittedly a less
well-defined figure, but based on a sales-volume assertion which seems
credible), plus a tad for Alpha Linux platforms, removes any questions about
even the possibly *marginal* nature of Alpha's profitability *even under the
existing not-so-benign neglect of its owner*.

3.  The fact that Compaq made no effort to market VMS and a
far-from-impressive effort to market Tru64 (and the resulting dampening
effect on customer confidence in the platform and in the vendor) means that
Alpha's profit potential was *at worst* significantly higher yet and indeed
had no well-definable upper bound.

I'm beginning to think that "crock of shit" was much too kind a
characterization, but I'll just let it stand.  And that's most of what was
in my mind when I said it, though I suspect floating around in the
background was the analysis we did of the 1999 annual report well over a
year ago.

"But wait!  All those figures were from a year ago, and we don't know
exactly what they meant even then!"  Kind of sounds like Dennis, who has
nothing to contribute to the conversation and apparently intensely resents
the fact that other people do...

Well, we're really not likely to be able to get much more detail out of
Compaq on this issue that's at all believable, but we can at least look at
associated figures from the same time and more recent ones as well to see
whether they seem consistent.

The 1999 annual report lists revenue and income figures for 1999, 1998, and
1997, bracketing the DEC acquisition.  These figures suggest that the DEC
portion of the Enterprise Solutions and Services portion of the business
(DEC, Tandem, and IA32 servers) contributed a little over half of its $20.1
billion in revenue - so imagining that VMS-related business contributed
about 1/3 of that DEC-related portion is not difficult (especially given
that Tru64 has been growing faster than VMS in the time since then and thus
may have accounted for less than 1/3 at that time).

[In that report, there's a moving sentiment from Mike Capellas that I'm sure
will go straight to the hearts of his Alpha customers:  "Above all, we are
passionate about being advocates for our customers.  That means listening to
them and responding to them.  This is the foundation upon which everything
else is built at Compaq."  But I digress...]

Enterprise Solutions and Services *income* for 1999 was a mere $2.3 billion,
however.  So while VMS-related sales accounted for only about 20% of the ESS
revenue, they seem to have accounted for about 35% of its profit.  You
shouldn't be surprised by this, since IIRC you've always asserted that VMS
was the high-margin leader in the Compaq product set.

What about other Compaq divisions in 1999?  Not so good, I'm afraid:  The
consumer-PC division had $6 billion in revenue and $260 million in income,
while the business-PC division managed to *lose* $450 million on $12.2
billion in revenue.  Oh, dear:  I guess that means that while VMS-related
business accounted for only a bit over 10% of overall Compaq revenue in
1999, it seems to have accounted for something close to 40% of the (gross)
overall Compaq profit.  As I said, Tru64 may have accounted for a bit less,
but still likely a fairly respectable figure.  Tandem was recently said to
contribute something like $1.3 billion in annual revenue, and I'd guess its
margins are similar to VMS's even if its gross is far smaller.  And, by
George, when you add all those up there's not a great deal left to account
for, so the numbers in fact seem to be consistent with the VMS numbers I
started out with - which isn't hard proof, but would need to be explained
away before asserting something else.

But one might still suggest that all this is ancient history and means
nothing (well, some people who had no better objection to offer might, I
guess).

The only 2000 annual report I could find (in the obvious location) was an
html fluff piece from Capellas.  But I did find an interesting document
entitled Restated_Data.pdf  that contains annual 1999 results modified to
reflect the subsequent separation of Enterprise Solutions and Services into
Enterprise Computing and Compaq Global Services and the merging of the
business and consumer PC divisions, plus quarterly results for all of 2000
using the same new structure.

It now becomes clear where the zero-dimensional minds at the Compaq helm got
the idea that services was the Great New Thing to concentrate on:
retroactively breaking out services in 1999 yielded $1 billion in income on
$7.4 billion in revenue, and the 2000 totals were similar ($884 million in
income on $7.5 billion in revenue).  Of course, a lot of that service
revenue is there only because the Compaq systems are there, but hey -
they're only accountants.

On the other hand, Enterprise Computing revenue (now without Services) grew
by 10% during the year 2000.  And while its 1999 (adjusted) income was only
$700 million (a figure that's hard to reconcile with the $1 billion
broken-out services contribution, since they don't add up to the
pre-break-up $2.3 billion figure:  I suspect they screwed up) on $13 billion
in revenue, its profits grew like gangbusters during 2000 (seems to
correlate with when Wildfire hit the streets) and the year closed out with
$1.7 billion in income on $14.3 billion in revenue.

"Economically unsustainable" my arse.

What of the combined consumer/business PC division?  Well, it pulled out of
the 1999 hole it was in (the pre-merge figure was about -$190 million, but
the merged figure was -$386 million - go figure) and managed to eke out $240
million in income on $20.7 billion in revenue.  By comparison, it seems like
an awful lot of work for not very much result.

The otherwise-largely-devoid-of-content annual report confirms this with the
observation:  "In fact, our enterprise businesses represented more than half
of our revenue and nearly 90 percent of business segment operating profit in
2000."

[Cue 2001 theme]

2001Q1 figures also appear in the above document, and they're not good -
except for services, which actually maintained its revenue and increased its
income by 10%.  Consumer/business PCs dropped back into the red, and
Enterprise Computing returned to its year-previous revenue and only half its
year-previous income.

So did the bottom really fall out of Alpha after all and justify (as least
from an extremely short-term perspective) its demise?  Nope:  the supporting
documentation (in a different document) for the Q1 figures says that
'storage and enterprise software' (which I guess means NSK, VMS, and Tru64,
since pretty much all the other 'enterprise software' got sold off a long
time ago) were continued strengths but that the main offsetting factor was a
major slump in industry-standard server revenue and income (an 'aggressive
pricing environment', which when the phrase has appeared in other - always
PC-related - contexts seems to be Compaq-speak for selling below effective
cost to maintain market share).

So here we are (in the Tijuana jail...):  PCs haven't contributed to
Compaq's profits for years and Intel servers are in a slump - so the obvious
solution is to get rid of the mainstay of Compaq's profits and get a cash
infusion to prop up those failing low-end businesses until they can drain
that cash away too.  'Incompetent' doesn't begin to describe it - and that's
without even considering the effect that it had on customers or the lies
about Alpha's continued ability to lead in performance (which are
consistently being refuted by the private comments of the engineers who
supposedly were the source of that fabrication - but that's another story).

Hope that's sufficient elucidation, 'cause I'm going to bed now.

- bill





