Jesus Fred, what the hell are you talking about?  We're not talking
about a processor that's scraping the bottom of the performance barrel
here.  EV6 is the fastest 64-bit processor in integer and floating
point performance, bar none.  Yes there have been delays in shrinks of
EV6, primarily associated with the switchover to a fabless model--but
even so, the current Alpha on the market is the fastest 64-bit
processor.

> Look how long it's taken to get the initial EV7s out
> there - it's not because Pete and his team couldn't design it.  

What is the reason, praytell?  You seem to have so much
information--feel free to email me if it is 'confidential'.

Look how long it took to get Willamette out, look how long it took to
get Merced out, look how long it took to get Power 4 out, look how
long it took get Mckinley out, look how long it took to get
Beast/Alien out, look how long it took to get any modern processor
out!

1. Processors are hard to design.
2. Processors take at least 6 years to design nowadays.
3. Alpha doesn't take any longer than anybody else.

But, the difference is:

4. When a new Alpha comes out, it takes the performance lead, and
usually it takes it away from the previous generation Alpha

> What's the
> cost per-chip going to be for EV7?  For EV8?

Well, we don't make 'em out of gold, if that's what you're asking.  We
use the same silicon and process technology as everybody else.  We
don't have the volumes of IA32, so the development cost is not
amortized over millions of parts--but I'm damn sure we have better
volumes than IA64 right now.  Would you like to compare cost per unit
of Merced to EV6? Mckinley to EV7?

And that's really the point.  Compaq business and marketing were not
able (willing?) to turn Alpha into a volume production, and they
didn't think they were going to be able to do so in the future.  Under
these constraints, the hardware business was not going to be
profitable--and cost/performance would suffer.  Actually, it isn't
quite this simple either, but that's the quick story.

It had absolutely nothing to do with Alpha engineers saying that we
weren't going to be able to keep up with IA64--which is a laughable
thought if you know how arrogant Alpha designers are ;-)

That is the only part of this whole thing that really pisses me off. 
The fact that I've heard from a dozen different sources that there are
people within Compaq saying that "this was initiated by Alpha
architects", and "they would not be able to sustain the performance
advantage" is just too much to handle.

