"JF Mezei" <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> wrote ...
> A former Digital employee, [...] mentioned that Intel would have high
> costs for IA64 because it requires greater amount of real estate and
> the odds of having a chip without defects capable of running at
> maximum speed would be much lower.
> I.E., the yields on IA64 would be much lower than on Alpha.

Well, before I start, I should mention there isn't always a
negative correlation between die size and yield.  One simple
example is redundant rows and columns on memory arrays:
makes the die larger, but improves %good-die enough that
even with fewer die per wafer, the number of good die per
wafer (the important metric) goes up.  Not that that's
necessarily the case here. That said:

> Could anyone comment on this ? Is that still true ? Is the yield an important
> enough variable that could make Alpha cheaper than IA64 ?

Not likely.  Now, I'm not speaking for Intel, or revealing any secrets,
The economics of it are pretty straight-forward.

First, you need to recognize that the economics of yield enhancement
depend on whether your chips are large or small, and whether they sell
for a lot of a little: if they are small and cheap it doesn't make as much
sense to invest in a lot of money in small reductions in defects per
wafer, but it does make a lot of sense if they are large and sell
for a nice price. If you assume each defect costs you one die, then
with small cheap die you're just losing a buck or two per wafer, and
maybe a 0.1% reduction in production capacity; but if you are
making large die that sell for a lot, each defect is costing you a
couple of hundred dollars, and (assuming times are good and your
production is wafer-start or perhaps tester limited) each defect
is costing you perhaps 0.5% of your production capacity, or more.

So all else being equal, a fab that's built to make big expensive die
will profit from investments in yield enhancement that would be
a waste of money for a fab making smaller cheaper chips.

Second, recognize the role that volume plays in the situation.
Regardless of how big your die is and how many more you
might be _able_ to produce by investing in yield enhancements,
if you can only sell, say, a paltry half-million or so of those
die every year, then again, big investments in yield enhancement
don't make any sense: it isn't amortized over enough units.

Third, recognize that fab processes are targeted for particular
applications: processors, ASICs, DRAM, SRAM, analogue:
all want from slightly to extremely different things from
a fab process.  Optimizing for one application domain usually
comes at the cost of some other application domain.

Now, look at Intel:  It sells a whole lot of microprocessors:
100's of millions. They range from big to very big in size.
They sell for anywhere from a decent piece of change to
a thick wad of cash.  Pretend you're running those fabs:
are you willing to spend millions on yield enhancements
that would make no sense for other manufacturers ?
Probably so, because the return on that investment in large.

Now, I'm not a naive amateur: I'm not about to make claims
that a process is "best" or "better", since it all depends on
what you're trying to do with it.  There are many companies
with great fabs.

But consider just the economics of it, and it's easy to believe
that Intel's fabs could be the most cost-effective high-volume
large-die processor fabs in the world.  Purely because
it is extremely good for the bottom line.
--
