"Fred Kleinsorge" <kleinsorge@star.zko.dec.com> wrote in message
news:OGG47.775$rc5.60253@news.cpqcorp.net...
> The pointer below gets an error about it not being found.

No problem for me right now, but I'll append it below for anyone who's
interested and couldn't retrieve it (perhaps I should have done so
originally, but the habit of using pointers when possible dies hard).

...

> I'm absolutely sure that you can and will find people who will argue that
> Alpha was superior to IA64.  In fact, I'll agree with that - to a point.
> You can build the most elegant, fastest, coolest thing in the world - but
if
> the dominant player is selling a million of them to every 10 that you
sell -
> you lose.

Not necessarily.  The critical factor is the amount that your higher cost
for that one *component* adds to your total *system* cost.

The cost of the Alpha processors in a current mid-range-or-up system does
not contribute very much to the total system cost (let alone TCO).  And
we've already been assured that future IA64 systems will have all the
goodies that current Alpha systems have - just different processors.  So
*even if* IA64 CPUs eventually have far higher volumes than Alphas (a future
that Compaq had the opportunity to challenge, since it's not clear that IA64
will ever do *anything* - save possibly for FP or crypto - better than one
or another of the available alternatives), that won't make a significant
difference to Alpha's price-attractiveness in its bread-and-butter markets -
so if Alpha offered noticeably better performance, it would still win there
(if marketed...).

...

> Given the resources I think we could have kept Alpha in the lead.  How
much
> in the lead... thats a tough question.

Which is why competition is a Good Thing - both for the species, and for (at
least most of) the individual products in it.

  How costly?  From all reports very.

It's been quantified:  we don't have to guess.  And the cost Winkler stated
($300 million annually) is well within Compaq's ability to make a handsome
profit on the result (it's not as if Compaq made so much higher profits in
other areas that this one appeared unattractive - rather the reverse).

> That's the bottom line.

Apparently not.  The bottom line appears to be that regardless of its profit
potential this was a business Compaq did not wish to be in.  That's an issue
for its shareholders, however:  the issue for its customers is the manner in
which it went about getting out of that business.

- bill

