In article <3b54659d@news.kapsch.co.at>,
Peter LANGSTOEGER <eplan@kapsch.net> wrote:
>Agreed.
>But if it happens, then it is too late for Q !
>Migrate VMS again to POWER ? Nobody will wait for it...

I wouldn't be surprised if they did. VMS customers tend to be locked into
VMS. OS migration is painful, architecture migration is a heck of a lot
less so. Numerous other operating systems have been forced into
incompatible CPU migrations in rapid succession because they chose
processors on the losing side of the performance wars; e.g. Stratus VOS 
found itself on the i860 (?) before moving to the HP-PA, and DG/UX was on 
the 88K before escaping to IA32.

An alternative is the path taken by Wang and a few other of the 
traditional mainframe/minicomputer vendors; keep your current processor, 
but stop chasing the performance curve. Every so often do a shrink, or add 
something newfangled like an L2 cache, and charge a heap of money for it.

I think that Digital for a while took both options with VMS -- didn't they
produce at least one "extra" generation of VAX after they released the
Alpha? The PDP-11 and PDP-10 lines found themselves with choice #2, and as 
a matter of fact I think that XKL is still making its TOAD PDP-10 clones, 
and there is at least one company out there producing blindingly fast 
PDP-11-on-a-PC-card systems. Or at least relatively blindingly fast.

Then there is the completely idiotic PR1ME approach, which was to keep
attempting to produce a processor somewhere close to the leading edge
until nearly bankrupt, then give up entirely. This left a fairly large
50-series customer base, who would probably have been willing to pay for
upgraded systems, up the creek without a paddle. [And given there were
more than enough customers that they made PrimeOS Y2k compliant 10 years
later, I think we can say definitively that completely abandoning the
hardware was a stupid move without being accused of armchair
quarterbacking.]

			Yours Truly,
			Jeffrey Boulier
