news:Lf237.13$XT4.47471@typhoon.ne.mediaone.net...

> Given the current Alpha installed base and shipment rate, and absent a
> DRAMATIC incease in adoption, Compaq really couldn't justify the continued
> investment. Decisions that were made in the early 1990s (let's build an
> architecture and not ensure that it becomes a volume platform) mid-90's
> (let's get suckered into a one-sided Alliance for Enterprise Computing)
and
> near the end of the decade (let's buy Digital and then marginalize its
> flagship architecture) have finally taken their toll.

If Alpha was such a dead end, why did Intel offer such concessions to Compaq
to drop Alpha?

Why didn't Intel simply setup recruiting offices in Nashua and Shrewsbury
and offer to hire the engineers that Compaq was going to have to layoff
because Alpha is going south and Compaq won't be able to keep it going?

Compaq had already laid off a significant number of hardware and software
engineers the preceding Friday and laid off even more the following Friday.
More will be laid off effective about 60 days from now, as well.  The costs
associated with these layoffs would simply speed up Compaq closing down
Alpha, making engineers available to Intel sooner.

My guess is that Intel found the risk that EV7 delivered at the same time as
McKinley would revitative interest in Alpha.  If the Linux performance of
EV7 systems exceeds McKinley systems of the same cost, and EV6 systems offer
performance equal to McKinley at a significantly lower cost, how rapidly
would Intel capture the 64 bit micro market?

AMD has demonstrated that it can survive selling chips for around 50% of
Intel's ASP ($90 vs $175 last quarter, $75 vs ???, this quarter).  This
leaves no margin for Intel to push IA64 to every server and then every
desktop.  Will IA64 be a success if fewer are sold per year than Sparc one
year from now?  Two years from now?  How embarrasing would it be for more
Alphas systems to be sold per year than IA64?

Clearly the risk is very high for Intel.  At the present time, Intel is
facing years until time to profit for IA64.

The Alpha program (which includes chips, systems, software, especially VMS,
services, etc.) reached profit within a few years of introduction and no one
has said that Alpha as a whole is unprofitable to Compaq.

Note, my argument does not depend on anyone agreeing that DEC should have
invested in Alpha?  If we agree that DEC could have invested the money spent
on Alpha into a different technology and gotten a higher return, that still
doesn't make IA64 a good investment.  I argue that Intel should have
invested in Alpha, instead of wasting its money on IA64 - Intel would be
making a significant profit on Alpha today if it had done so.




