hi:

I was looking for a place to hype my new book - The Unix Guide to
Defenestration ; see my winface.com site - but got sidetracked into
reading all the postings on the the IA64 volume/dominance issue.
Weird, does no-one remember the pentium-pro?

That chip was in many ways Intel's first attempt at a full 32bit CPU
environment and, like today's Xeons, didn't do dumb things like run
"native" 16bit instructions. It was a great product -one of the few
times that Intel produced something that was performance competitive -
but it almost destroyed Intel's desktop monopoly because it allowed
companies like AMD to gain market share by creating high megahertz
32/16bit chips that beat the pants off the Pentium Pro in running
Windows 3.1 and 95 applications with 16bit instruction sets.

Will the Itantic's lack of backwards compatibility have the same
effect? Personally I think it will, but it is clear that Intel and
Microsoft have
something different in mind. It's no coincidence - at least I don't
think it is- that the alphacide announcement came on the same day that
the appeals court
set aside the penalities Judge Jackson had assessed against Microsoft.

What I think they expect is that this time the license subscription
strategy being embarked on by Microsoft will kill off that old 32bit
code and AMD with
it.  Whether that is the plan, and whether it works or not, are things
that
remain to be seen but the Itanic has promise - and patent protection -
so
it will be interesting to watch.


