> > information from the drive itself.
> > 
> > If you wanted it bootable, you would instead do something like:
> > 
> > 	disklabel -t advfs -wr dsk1 dsk_dynamic_geometry
> > 
> > or
> > 
> > 	disklabel -t ufs -wr dsk1 dsk_dynamic_geometry
> > 
> > > >/sbin/scu -f /dev/rdisk/dsk1a format
> > > >
> > > >OK, its formated now.
> > 
> > Ok.
> > 
> > > I have the Compaq Non-Commercial Tru64 UNIX V5 and can't use AdvFS. 
> > 
> > Why can't you use AdvFS?  I have the Tru64 Non-Commercial V 5.0a at home,
> > and AdvFS is a filesystem option for me.
> > 
> > > I can't use addvol its need the license too.
> > > 
> > > 5.
> > > >/sbin/disklabel -wr -t UFS /dev/rdisk/dsk1a
> > > >/sbin/disklabel -wr -t UFS /dev/rdisk/dsk1g
> > 
> > Ok, you just wrote a default label, twice.  disklabel just creates the
> > partition table at the beginning of the disk -- other than setting the
> > boundaries of each partition, it doesn't do anything to any of the partitions
> > themselves.  You only write a disklabel to a disk once.  You do LSM or
> > filesystem to the partitions, but the label is at the head of the disk
> > and is not part of any of the partitions.
> > 
> > > 6.
> > > >/sbin/newfs /dev/rdisk/dsk1g
> > > 
> > > Its creating the new file system....
> > 
> > Ok.
> > 
> > > I tried /sbin/mount -d /dev/rdisk/dsk1g /data (for e.g.)
> > 
> > Since you're using UFS, and the filesystem has never been mounted, it's
> > not in a known clean state.  It's a common idiom among the commercial UNIXes
> > to do
> > 
> > 	newfs ...
> > 	fsck ...
> > 	mount ...
> > 
> > to fsck the filesystem right after it's created, before it's mounted the
> > first time.
> > 
> > Once you've fscked it, just
> > 
> > 	mount /dev/disk/dsk1g /data
> > 
> > Note you do *not* want the raw device (/dev/rdisk/dsk1g) for mount --
> > mount should always work through the *block* device.  Newfs and fsck
> > work through the raw device, but mount does not.
> > 
