from Compaq employees supporting Compaq's position.  I'm sure some of them
are simply trying to do the best with the hand Compaq has dealt them (which
they likely have absolutely no control over), and if I'm a bit blunt in
responding to some of their more dubious assertions I hope they'll
understand that I'm not mad at them personally.

However, we're not hearing from employees on the other side of the issue,
since they might well consider public airing of their feelings to be a
Career-Limiting Move - though here's an exception in comp.arch, who's
responding to an indirect report of another exception (both from Alpha
engineers refuting the assertion that Alpha might have had difficulty
keeping up with the IA64 performance-wise, and especially the assertion that
it was the Alpha engineers themselves who told Compaq this):

news:4495ef1f.0107131853.68b45faa@posting.google.com

Anyway:  This post is an invitation to Compaq employees who have something
they'd like to say on this issue (but are reluctant to do so for
career-related reasons) to find some non-Compaq poster to one of the
included newsgroups whom they know and trust and convey their sentiments
indirectly.

I'll be happy to act as a conduit for anyone I know, or anyone vouched for
as being credible by someone I know (though after my 15-year absence from
DEC that population is likely pretty small).  But I'm not in a position to
be able to verify statements (or even Compaq identity) from people I have no
direct or indirect knowledge of, and unlike Compaq I'm at least a little
picky in the qualify of the information I disburse.

Anyone who doesn't know someone who can act as a conduit can of course still
contribute using 'anonymizer'-type facilities, but the lack of any kind of
assurance of identity will mean that your post must meet a pretty high
standard of credibility to avoid being sloughed off as competitive FUD.

I don't know whether the above is a good idea or a lousy one, but the nice
thing about it is that it doesn't matter:  if it's a good idea, people will
respond; if not, it'll just die quietly.

