"Brannon Batson" <Brannon_Batson@yahoo.com> wrote ...
> I'll just say that it is an extremely unpleasant experience to have
> your project cancelled

You get used to it.  Anyone who wants to lead in this
industry has to try things that don't pan out.  IME,
the usual mistake isn't that a project gets cancelled,
but that it doesn't get cancelled soon enough.

I worked on one project that got cancelled two days after
requesting the funding to get masks made and produce
first silicon.  Was that the right thing to do ? Probably.
Events just prior to that time had made it clear that there
was a significant amount of non-technical  heavy lifting
that would have to be done to make the product successful,
and difficult if not insurmountable psychological barriers
to overcome before end users would accept the product.
We couldn't know all that when the project started, but
we knew it before the project was cancelled.

Things change: economies boom and bust, new ideas
sweep the marketplace (like FPS games did years ago),
and if you're doing your job right, your own knowledge
of things gets better over time.  So sometimes a project
you think is hot in 1999 become a project you don't have
any objection to them canceling in 2000.

A company that never cancels projects is either too
conservative to maintain leadership, or doomed to
disaster when conditions change suddenly.  And
there's no denying, BTW, that the market for
computers has recently changed suddenly.

> [...] you should never trust anyone who wears a
> tie to work.

Frankly, I have no problem trusting the suits to do
_their_ job, if they'll trust me to do mine.  (Especially
Intel's suits, who have quite a record of accomplishment,
even if there have been a few mistakes.)  It's their
job to analyze the market and it's future and decide
which markets to pursue, the strategy to use to
pursue that market, and the financials.  It's my job
to contribute to the team that makes the products
that lead to the success of those decisions.

One shouldn't try to control everything.  One should
just do your best on the things you can control, and try
to have some influence on the things that you can't.
And not stress over it when things don't go your way,
especially if you still have a job.

> I honestly hope that it never happens to you, but you
> are kidding yourself if you think that SGI is immune from it.

Certainly Intel isn't ... and that's a good thing.
But of course, I'm not speaking for Intel.
--
