In article <9im1vj$4eh$1@nnrp2.phx.gblx.net>, "Dennis O'Connor" <dmoc@primenet.com> writes:
> "mulp" <michaelpettengill@earthlink.net> wrote ...
>> And my belief is that the day after the product ships, you continue testing
> 
> As opposed to the Microsoft belief, that once you've shipped,
> the customers will do your testing for you ...
> 
>> > If you really wanted to determine whether there were any bugs
>> > in a system or not, testing is no substitute for formal verification.
>> > However, formal verification is not widely used AFAIK, and I'm
>> > not sure why ... maybe common computers aren't fast enough to
>> > do it ... or maybe they don't have enough virtual memory space ... :-)
>>
>> Have you every tried to specify a real system so that it can be verified?
>> Systems involving multiple components that operate asynchronously are quite
>> difficult.
> 
> But so is thoroughly testing them, right ?  But as I said, I don't know why
> formal verification isn't more widely used.

Formal verification won't detect a faulty specification.

A human tester "say, this new utility program does not have a HELP
command like all the other programs on this platform".

If you spend all your money on formal verification, you won't
have any left for the informal testing that is an absolute requirement.

Companies also are unwilling to force use of the programming languages
most suitable to formal verification.  The last software product I
remember that underwent formal verification was DEC's SVS A1 Monitor.
It never made it to market because part way through field test they
discovered that the folks in the US government who made the purchase
decisions were disjoint from the folks in the US government who wanted
A1.  Even the NSA runs "system high".


