    Larry> Formal verification won't detect a faulty specification.

Oh yes it will. "90% of the errors found in a formal 
verification are usually errors in the specification." (A quote from
someone who worked on an fv project at Intel.)  

    Larry> The last software product I remember that underwent formal
    Larry> verification was DEC's SVS A1 Monitor.  

The last one I remember, just in Dec, was (1996-7) of the cache
coherence protocol for a system code-named wildfire.  See 
http://www.research.compaq.com/SRC/personal/lamport/pubs/wildfire-challenge.ps
Of course that is not a verification of an entire product; and of course
it doesn't address whether what is specified would be any good.  But 
the sharper you know what a thing actually is, the better you can 
judge whether it would be any good. 

Peter Hancock


