In article <9imq0m$s37$8@bob.news.rcn.net>, jmfbahciv@aol.com writes:
> In article <r5YQ40C5Zv70@eisner.encompasserve.org>,
>    Kilgallen@eisner.decus.org.nospam (Larry Kilgallen) wrote:

>>What constitutes "reading the code" ?  There is a continuum
>>from formal inspection down to just measuring the total lines
>>of code (to make a guess at the test effort required).
> 
> [emoticon gapes, rereads the last one, gapes...repeated until
> gaping factor was diminished so that jaw didn't hit floor]
> You have got to be shitting me.  You can make a prediction 
> based on number of lines of code...I can't even define the
> term "number of lines of code".  How....????

Well run organizations make predictions of defect counts based on
lines of code all the time.  There are other elements that should
be included as well, but lines of code is not irrelevant.  Surely
you don't feel that a 1000 line program has the same number of
total defects as a 100,000 line program (all other things being
equal).

There are standard methods of counting lines of code, and tools
that do it.  When comparing programs, it does not really matter
what your rules are for "lines of code", it only matters that
you use the same rules for all programs.

>>  One
>>such technique falling in the middle ground, for a certain
>>language that shall remain nameless, is to search for any
>>line that contains a single equal sign ("=") and also the
>>lexeme "if" :-)
> 
> 
> <grin>  Well, there were those little tricks of the trade.
> One of my den mother tests was to feed an EXE file as input
> to something expecting ASCII.  The fit always hits the shan
> with that one.

Yes, that is much cheaper than examining the source to look for
weak spots. Lots of techniques are applicable, but some are quite
a bit cheaper to implement.

I believe the overwhelming reason why software is not tested enough
is that testing is perceived as being too expensive.


