<JeffKenton@mediaone.net> wrote:

>Dave Hansen wrote:
>> 
>	...
>> 
>> You test to find bugs.  You have limited resources, so you want to
>> perform the tests that are most likely to find bugs.  You run
>> regression tests because empirical evidence shows that bugs that have
>> been "fixed" tend to "reappear" in later revisions.  Therefore,
>> regression tests are likely to find bugs.
>> 
>
>Of course.  But this begs the question of what's important when testing a new
>piece of software.  Do you test weird but unimportant cases, or do you think
>about what matters most?
>
>I saw some compiler testers find out that subroutine calls with more than 255
>parameters would fail, but they neglected to notice that one of the basic
>arithmetic operators wasn't implemented.  You need to add judgment to your
>automation.
>

You seem to think I'm saying something that I'm not.  I'm not sure
what it is, maybe "All bugs have equal importance" or "Testers
shouldn't waste their time on something the programmers should have
tested themselves" (although, you *have* to admit that omitting a
basic arithmetic operator from a compiler is pretty egregious.  ;-)

But my main point is that the purpose of testing is *not* to prove the
software is correct.  1) It can't.  It's just not possible.  2) It
sets the tester up for failure: if he finds a bug, he's failed to
prove the software is correct.

My response above was to the assertion that "regression testing is a
case where the purpose of testing is to verify software meets its
requirements."  No, it's not.  The purpose of regression testing is to
find "fixed" bugs that have reappeared.  This is a very common class
of bug, and therefore regression testing is likely to find bugs.  If
it was not likely to find bugs, it would not often be done.

As to your compiler example, it seems hard to imagine a competent test
team that wouldn't at least stumble on the fact that a particular
operator was missing.  I don't know what language this was, but with C
there are so many neat little precedence and promotion tricks that you
can try, along with operations that are legal only with certain types,
that it must show up somewhere.  I think it falls into the class of
"low-hanging fruit" to which Bill Todd referred in a different post.

Regards,

                               -=Dave
