host -portable
> -readonly_strings -msg_disable inlinestoclsmod,valuepres -c ltdl.c  -DPIC
>
> *why* does the compiler generate an Info message regarding the assignment
> above (and subsequent Warning messages about ptrmismatch when func is
> used later):
>
> cc: Info: ltdl.c, line 2557: In the initializer for func, "data1" of type
> "pointer to void", is being converted to "pointer to function (pointer to
> const char, pointer to void) returning int".  Such a cast is not permitted
by
> the standard. (nonstandcast)
>         = (int (*) LT_PARAMS((const char *filename, lt_ptr data))) data1;
> -------------------------------------------------------------------^
>

    The compiler emits the message because you specified -portable on the
    command line.  This tells the compiler to emit diagnositics for
constructs
    that may not be accepted by other compilers.  Because this cast violates
    the C standard, other  compilers may not accept it.

> It's nice that it says that `Such a cast is not permitted by the
standard.',
> but I'm left wondering what the real problem is.

    The standard only permits casts between pointers to object or incomplete
    types and other pointers to object or incomplete types, or between
pointers
    to function types.  One can not cast a pointer to void (an incomplete
type) to a pointer to a
    function type (as you are doing).  I don't want to guess why the
standard
    does not allow this.  Maybe you could ask in comp.lang.c.

    Note that if you would like more information on any compiler message,
    add the -verbose switch to the command line.  This will cause additional
    information to be output for each message.

    Hope this helps,

