> >> intent of finding errors."  Seems obvious until you find out how many
> >> people don't believe it.
> >
> >What do those people believe ?   It is a way to attract the Easter bunny ?
> 
> "Testing is the process of demonstrating errors are not present."
> 
> "The purpose of testing is to show that a program performs its
> intended functions correctly"
> 
> "Testing is the process of establishing confidence that a program does
> what it is supposed to do."
> 
> These are all examples from Myers' book.  I've heard the last two
> (almost word-for-word) vigorously argued by several different people.
> Other examples I've encountered:
> 
> "Testing is the process of validating software."
> 
> "Testing is verifying that a program meets its requirements."
> 
> And on and on...

Perhaps what's going on here, or perhaps not, is missing a
distinction -- if there is any -- between testing and
certification. (Gee, I'm real certain of myself, aren't I? :-) )

Or more generally, between multiple levels of "purpose" and
"intention" of the testing.

What I have in the back of my head here are the tests being
defined in InfiniBand to define compliance with the
specification. There are compliance statements in the spec. A
workgroup of the association makes up (is making up) a collection
of tests that verify that a particular unit under test conforms
to all those compliance statements. If the box passes all the
tests, it is deemed compliant.

In this case, the explicitly stated top-level purpose of that
testing is branding.

A necessary sub-goal is finding errors. If the tests aren't
constructed to find errors, they don't work for the purpose of
branding.

And the reason somebody wants the branding is that s/he wants to
ship a product.

There are always levels of purpose in almost every activity.

Greg Pfister


