:-) 

Sometimes that idea that is so clear in my head is so hard to express
in writing, for all to see and critique.

>
>Or more generally, between multiple levels of "purpose" and
>"intention" of the testing.

Yes.  Or rather the idea that certification *is* testing, and vice
versa.  I hold it is not -- they are two different activites.  The
product of testing is (should be) used as an input to certification,
but testing itself is not certification.

>
>What I have in the back of my head here are the tests being
>defined in InfiniBand to define compliance with the
>specification. There are compliance statements in the spec. A
>workgroup of the association makes up (is making up) a collection
>of tests that verify that a particular unit under test conforms
>to all those compliance statements. If the box passes all the
>tests, it is deemed compliant.

I would put it slightly differently.

The workgroup is making up a collection of tests whose purpose is to
find incompatibilities (bugs) in an implementation.  If the tests fail
to find any such incompatibility, the unit will be said to have passed
the compatibility tests.

Deeming the unit "compliant" is (perhaps) dangerous.  Consider that by
doing so, the tests become an alternate, perhaps "superior"
specification ("Superior" because there's little argument about
whether a particular implementation complies: It passes or it doesn't.
"Alternate" because it may differ in subtle or substantial ways from
the intent or letter of the specification).

>
>In this case, the explicitly stated top-level purpose of that
>testing is branding.

Like Microsoft's "Designed for Microsoft Windows xx" program.

>
>A necessary sub-goal is finding errors. If the tests aren't
>constructed to find errors, they don't work for the purpose of
>branding.

Indeed, it's worse than that.  If two units from two companies pass
the tests, and are thus deemed "compliant," yet don't play well
together, who gets to fix it?  Whose unit is "wrong?"

>
>And the reason somebody wants the branding is that s/he wants to
>ship a product.
>
>There are always levels of purpose in almost every activity.

And some of the "levels" have conflicting goals.  "Faster, smaller,
cheaper: choose any two."  My point is to get the conflicting cruft
out of the way: Testers should find bugs.  Leave the "good enough"
decisions to someone else.  

In terms of your Infiniband example, I would explicity state that the
goal of the testing is to find incompatibilities (or bugs) rather than
"prove" a unit is "compliant."  Indeed, tests can *only* prove
non-compliance.

