Whoa!  You misunderstand my understanding :-).  A part of
the value of those bananas is that the customer may buy
something else that brings in a huge profit.  And that
can't doesn't seem to be a concern with those financial 
types.  In the case of TOPS-10 a long time ago, the 
customers who had a PDP-10 on site, seemed to also have
bought other gear/software of the other product lines
which was highly profitable.  However, that profit margin
didn't get counted as PDP-10-influenced.  The figure that
I seem to remember is about ~70% of all the other product
line business was done with the customers who also had
PDP-10s.  Nobody (that I heard of) took a look at which
came first.  

Also, if a kiddie cut his teeth on a university (or it's
high school these days) system running VMS, when he grows
up and gets to be in charge of influencing procurements,
he's going to want to to stick with the product he knows.
It used to be called goodwill, an aspect that seems to have
disappeared in business.

Now, if I find a store that always carries top-quality 
bananas, I'm going to buy all of my other groceries at that
store because I foolishly believe that the same quality
standards will be used with the other wares of the grocery
store.

/BAH



