About the time that the 10 line was being killed off was also around the
time that the MBAs were busy with their calculators.  The classic engineers
responded to the bean counters by totalling up the complete contribution to
the corporation.  That's when the MBAs started all the restructuring because
when they totalled up the contributions from the 10, 11s, VAX, services,
consulting, layered products, and so on, they got a total revenue for DEC
that exceeded $50 billion, a figure that was about 4 times higher than the
actual revenue.  This resulted in MBA driven restructuring to make it easy
to analyze the business.

Rando (head of services) was a master at capturing the revenue and profit so
that within a few years his organization was responsible for half the
revenue and in the bad years 80-200% of the profit.  One way that this was
done was by charging high service contract rates for VAX hardware and
software - this was possible because there was a broad set of products and
services related to VAX - for customers the VAX related products and
services were sufficiently better than the alternatives so that they paid
the high margin fees.

For new customers and ISVs, VAX had no relevance; it came down to what was
going on with Alpha.  Since Rando was measured by how well he did, not how
well DEC did, he was discouraged from spending the VAX margin to develop
Alpha business, especially when his management had set Windows as their
strategic direction.  So, Rando spend money on Windows related development
and lots and lots of Rando's people switched from VMS to Windows - few VMS
folk were interested in working on unix since it didn't seem to have a
better future than VMS.

Ah, one of the things that existed until the late 80s was the idea of
"central engineering" with product lines funding their own engineering when
central engineering didn't deliver the products they needed.  Central
engineering was purely a cost; project funding was determined in part by the
revenue contribution of the resulting products.  Given a project that would
spend $200K on RSX-11M development and a second project that would spend
$500K on a VMS layered product, the VMS project would win because the PDP-11
contribution to DEC was $1 billion while the VMS related contribution was $6
billion (and the 10 related $800 million).  (PDP-11 related projects would
win over PDP-10 because there were more product lines selling PDP-11 stuff
while the 10s were limited to just the LCG product line.)

The interesting thing about the PDP-11 stuff was that it had the highest
margins all around.  For the small software product set, the margins were
60-80%, for the hardware service probably 30-40%, for the hardware sales,
probably 60-80%.  And the margins today are still high enough to justify
designing new PDP-11 CPUs to replace the old designs that can't be
manufactured today because there are no ICs of the type required.

In fact, this is something to consider.  PDP-11s have been built out of
expensive parts (FPGAs, etc) even when micros with better performance are
available for less than $5.  The PDP-11 market isn't growing, but it is a
profitable niche market even with crippling CPU chip costs.




