Here's a chance for me to show how rusty I am at C.  ;-)

Given the following (preprocessed) snippet, starting a function:


static int
foreachfile_callback (dirname, data1, data2)
     char *dirname;
      void *  data1;
      void *  data2;
{
  int (*func)  ( const char * filename , void * data ) 
	= (int (*)  ( const char * filename , void * data ) ) data1;


When compiling this on Tru64 UNIX 5.1 with 

Compaq C V6.3-028 on Compaq Tru64 UNIX V5.1 (Rev. 732)
Compiler Driver V6.3-026 (sys) cc Driver

and the following flags:

cc -DHAVE_CONFIG_H -I. -I. -I. -std1 -O2 -g3 -tune host -arch host -portable
-readonly_strings -msg_disable inlinestoclsmod,valuepres -c ltdl.c  -DPIC

*why* does the compiler generate an Info message regarding the assignment
above (and subsequent Warning messages about ptrmismatch when func is
used later):

cc: Info: ltdl.c, line 2557: In the initializer for func, "data1" of type
"pointer to void", is being converted to "pointer to function (pointer to
const char, pointer to void) returning int".  Such a cast is not permitted by
the standard. (nonstandcast)
        = (int (*) LT_PARAMS((const char *filename, lt_ptr data))) data1;
-------------------------------------------------------------------^


It's nice that it says that `Such a cast is not permitted by the standard.',
but I'm left wondering what the real problem is.  K&R2e has a bit of
vague verbiage on page 103 regarding, "...or even, except for void *, to
assign a pointer of one type to a pointer of another type without a cast."

Well, it *is* a void * that's being converted to a pointer to a function,
so it seems to be perfectly in line with what K&R2e says.

Is it spelled out more clearly in some subsequent standard (C89, or C9X)
why this is "not permitted by the standard"?

Thanks,

Tim
