> In article <3b4e1699.92129993@News.CIS.DFN.DE>,
>  on Thu, 12 Jul 2001 21:49:00 GMT,
>  iddw@hotmail.com (Dave Hansen) writes:
> >I warned you this was a hot-button...  ;-)

...

> >The reason I'm so adamant about it is that any other definition sets
> >you (or rather, the tester) up for failure.  If the tester can't
> >fulfill his purpose, or worse, by finding bugs *thwarts* his stated
> >purpose, he's failed.
>
>          What if the tester doesn't find any bugs because there
> aren't any?  Does that mean he failed?

Nope.  Perhaps you'd be more comfortable with the statement "The purpose of
testing is to find any bugs that exist", but in practice the difference is
questionable.

>
> >And by focusing the tester on what *can* be done allows him to do a
> >better job than he otherwise could have.  If your purpose is to find
> >bugs, that is what you'll (try to) do.  If your purpose is to prove
> >the software is correct, *that* is what you'll (try to) do.  Testers
> >should not be worried about determining whether the software is good
> >enough to ship.  They should worry about finding bugs.
>
>          I don't agree.  The tester who only checks unimportant
> cases is not doing his job properly even if he finds a whole
> bunch of bugs.

I'm not sure where you got the impression that the definition excluded
'important' cases, but I certainly didn't see it in there.

If the tester's purpose is to find bugs, they'll likely go for the
low-hanging fruit first (just as is true in most other endeavors).  And
there's nothing in the definition to prevent getting hints about cases of
special importance to cover:  its main purpose is to ensure that finding
bugs is viewed positively rather than as some kind of necessary evil.

- bill

