Paul DeMone <pdemone@igs.net> writes:

>Most ECAD/EDA tool vendors have never seriously embraced Alpha. Part
>of it is a cost consideration out of the chicken-and-the-egg dilemma
>of a low marketshare ISA. But I think an important secondary reason
>is because with a per CPU license pricing scheme it is in the vendor's
>vested interest to keep its user base on the platform with the weakest
>processors, namely SPARC.


That's an interesting idea, but I'm skeptical.  Mentor Graphics, for
instance, while they kept their software available on Apollo Domain/OS
after HP swallowed them and introduced the 400 series (capable of
running both Domain/OS and HP-UX, on 68030 and later 68040 CPUs) also
was pretty quick to port to the much faster PA-RISC based workstations
introduced not long after.  Maybe they are atypical though, I wasn't
familiar with all the players at the time and what they were doing.

I think it was more a factor of the software starting on Sun and Apollo
because they were first out the gate with realistic workstations that
could be used for this purpose, so the software followed them from
68K to Sparc and PA-RISC.  Any initial marketshare DEC had with Ultrix
(I don't know what the situation was there) would have been seriously
hurt because of the discontinuity between Ultrix and OSF/1.  HP seems
to have managed the migration of another company's customers from a
non-Unix OS (granted with a pretty convincing Unix personality) better
than DEC managed a migration of their own customers from one Unix to
another.  They handled the VMS side far better.  Perhaps a 'VEST'
equivalent for MIPS/Ultrix could have really helped.

There are probably still a fair number of Apollos chugging away in
the EDA world, due to the difficulty of moving Mentor 7 designs into
Mentor 8.  I know there are two still running I helped set up in late
1994 (that were consolidated from the 15 or so running before) at my
first foray in the corporate world.  They port the designs from there
as needed for customer support work and such, but economically it is
easier to keep those two machines (they are twins, so if one fails
they are fine, if both fail they may have some problems) going than
to pay someone to get everything converted before it is necessary.

