"Terry C. Shannon" <terryshannon@mediaone.net> wrote in message
news:Lf237.13$XT4.47471@typhoon.ne.mediaone.net...
> Given the current Alpha installed base and shipment rate, and absent a
> DRAMATIC incease in adoption, Compaq really couldn't justify the continued
> investment. Decisions that were made in the early 1990s (let's build an
> architecture and not ensure that it becomes a volume platform) mid-90's
> (let's get suckered into a one-sided Alliance for Enterprise Computing)
and
> near the end of the decade (let's buy Digital and then marginalize its
> flagship architecture) have finally taken their toll.

It wasn't so much a matter of failing to make Alpha volume, as much as
making sure that VMS revenue declined in the hopes that it would go away and
somehow make Digital Unix successful.

Compaq has diluted the Tandem uniqueness as well.

The problem is that customers and ISVs would not cooperate and today there
is a massive amount of revenue and profit/profit potential in the VMS and
Tandem businesses.

A few changes in the slides replaces Alpha with IA64 and magically a lot of
money is saved in the engineering costs.  But on the other hand, the revenue
coming from the chip and system sales goes away as well.   Maybe $250
million was spent per year, but the net cost was a lot less.  So with a cost
of switching to a new architecture not having been calculated, but my swag
puts it at at least half a billion.  So the switch reaches break even at
best 10 years down the road, assuming that Compaq stays in business that
long.

And if you say, "but Intel is going pay for the porting", I say, well, they
should pay for the half billion in porting because this is to their
advantage, but they should also pay a billion or two for the IP that they at
getting in the deal.  My guess is that Intel is only going pay a half
billion, but somehow that is going to end up being in reduced Intel chip
prices which will mean that the Intel side of the business will be more
competitive with Dell while the VMS, Tandem, and Tru64 businesses struggle
with the lost customers and ISVs.

In the end, Dell will go to Intel and say, give us the same price you give
Compaq or we switch to AMD.




