Ah, time to get out the Charlie Matco Secret Decoder Ring. Sorry about the
codename confusion.

IPF = Itanium Processor Family. First generation is the current Itanium,
followed by McKinley, Madison, and Deerfield.

Marvel = the Alpha EV7-based successor to the current GS80-GS320 "WildFire"
server. Up to 64 CPUs, modular design, leverages EV7 "glueless SMP"
interconnect strategy to reduce cost and improve scalability.

InfiniBand - a new low latency high bandwidth I/O and interconnect fabric.
It incorporates many of the concepts of Compaq's Tandem-developed ServerNet
architecture. InfiniBand will show up first as an I/O subsystem in ProLiant
systems, and then will be adopted as a cluster interconnect. It is good
stuff.

QuickBlade, BladeRunner, etc. Code names for systems that will rely on CPU
modules (called Blades) that sit on an InfiniBand "backplane." RLX
Technologies, which was formed early this year by renegades from the Houston
Server Mafia (ProLiant developers) also has a blade strategy and product
offering. Compaq's ISSG will debut QuickBlade-based IA-32 systems late this
year. The High Performance Systems Division won't have an equivalent
offering until ~2004. That will be the IPF-Inside successor to the
Alpha-based Marvel enterprise server.


cheers,

terry s








