>
> Dear Jeff,
>
> This is the first in a series of "Tech Talks" by Terry Shannon.  This Tech
> Talk will be on IA-64: Good Questions You Will Need to Answer
>
> We live in interesting times. Compaq and Intel on June 25 announced that
> Alpha, long the fastest processor on the planet, would reach its
production
> omega with the ~2004 introduction of the Alpha 21364 EV79 part. Compaq
> subsequently will base all its enterprise systems on forthcoming members
of
> Intel's IPF (Itanium Processor Family), a.k.a. IA-64, microprocessor
lineup.
> Under the terms of the non-exclusive agreement-an agreement that
represents
> an undeniable coup for Intel-Compaq will consolidate its entire 64-bit
> platform and OS lineup on the IPF architecture beginning in 2004. In the
> near term, nothing will change. New Alpha systems and upgrades will be
> rolled out through 2004, and all incumbent systems will be supported
through
> 2012. Compaq already has started porting NSK, Tru64 UNIX, and VMS to the
IPF
> while transferring key Alpha processor and compiler technology, tools, and
> engineering resources to Intel.
>
> They've Killed Alpha! Why?
>
> Compaq really didn't have much choice. Economic realities played a big
role
> in the decision to terminate Alpha chip development upon the completion of
> the EV79 processor. Do the math: based on an estimated ship rate of ~100K
> Alpha systems per year, Compaq's Alpha chip consumption was ~250K to ~500K
> CPUs per year. Given Alpha design and development costs of ~$250M per
year,
> fixed overhead added as much as $1K to the cost of each Alpha chip Compaq
> shipped. Absent a dramatic increase in AlphaServer adoption rates, Alpha
was
> indicted by the law of diminishing returns, and tried and convicted in the
> court of the marketplace. What's more, SKC believes Compaq became privy to
> McKinley performance information that drove a stake through the heart of
the
> Alpha "we're twice as fast as anyone else" value proposition.
>
> So, Will IPF Be Alpha-Inside?
>
> Good question! Given the radical differences in the Alpha superscalar RISC
> and the IPF EPIC architectures, it's uncertain exactly which pieces of
Alpha
> technology can be incorporated into future IPF chips. (Indeed, the true
> unification of a RISC and EPIC architecture would be the Mother of All
> Architectural Shotgun Marriages.) Intel will likely capitalize on the
> on-chip memory controller, interprocessor communications interfaces, and
> on-chip routing technology that EV7 brings to the table; whether IPF
> designers can leverage the EV8 processor's simultaneous multithreading
> design remains to be seen. With McKinley a done deal the question becomes
> what will the Compaq impact be on the third-generation Madison IPF chip
the
> generation after Deerfield IPF chip. How will Compaq execute its strategy
> before it can fully impact IPF chip design?
>
> Wildfire, Marvel, then What?
>
> Mongo systems based on CPU Blades and an InfiniBand fabric, just as Compaq
> planned to deliver.  The High Performance Systems Division began designing
a
> post-Marvel processor-neutral enterprise server that leverages CPU blades
> and InfiniBand fabric well over a year ago, with a projected availability
in
> the middle of the decade. (Those familiar with the ISSG's BladeRunner
> architecture and the forthcoming QuickBlade server can consider the
> enterprise server to be "QuickBlade on Steroids."  Since the platform was
> designed from the ground up to be architecture-neutral, the IPF-Inside
> strategy will not impact the design team. Freed from the burden of
> developing-and funding-Alpha CPUs, Compaq can focus more resources on
> system-level enhancements and product differentiators such as RAS, common
> management tools, middleware, advanced partitioning and resource
management
> capabilities, etc-in short, all the underpinnings for the firm's long-term
> Server Utility vision.
>
