Revised...

This is the first in a series of "Tech Talks" by Terry
Shannon. This Tech Talk will be on IA-64: Good Questions
You Will Need to Answer

We live in interesting times. Compaq and Intel on June 25
announced that Alpha, long the fastest processor on the
planet, would reach its production omega with the ~2004
introduction of the Alpha 21364 EV79 part. Compaq
subsequently will base all its enterprise systems on
forthcoming members of Intel's IPF (Itanium Processor
Family), a.k.a. IA-64, microprocessor lineup. Under the
terms of the non-exclusive agreement--an agreement that
represents an undeniable coup for Intel--Compaq will
consolidate its entire 64-bit platform and OS lineup on the
IPF architecture beginning in 2004. In the near term,
nothing will change. The AlphaServer and OS roadmap remain
intact and essentially unchanged through 2003, and Compaq
will continue to build and sell Alpha systems through 2013,
and as long thereafter as sufficient demand exists. (Alpha
products will be supported for at least five years beyond
their final purchase date, so ongoing support is assured
through 2018.) Compaq already has started porting NSK,
Tru64 UNIX, and VMS to the IPF while transferring key Alpha
processor and compiler technology, tools, and engineering
resources to Intel.

They've Killed Alpha! Why?

Given technical and economic realities, Compaq really
didn't have much choice. A detailed technical assessment of
the IPF and Alpha product roadmaps revealed that IPF
products would in the reasonably near term marginalize
Alpha�s �sustainable performance advantage� value
proposition. Although primarily a technical decision,
Compaq�s announcement that it will terminate Alpha
development upon the completion of the EV79 CPU has an
economic component as well. Do the math: based on an
estimated ship rate of ~100K Alpha systems per year,
Compaq's Alpha chip consumption was ~250K to ~500K CPUs per
year. Given Alpha design and development costs of ~$250M
per year, fixed overhead added as much as $1K to the cost
of each Alpha chip Compaq shipped. Absent a dramatic
increase in AlphaServer adoption rates, Alpha was indicted
by the law of diminishing returns, and tried and convicted
in the court of the marketplace.

So, Will IPF Be Alpha-Inside?

It�s uncertain exactly which pieces of Alpha technology
actually can be incorporated into future IPF chips, but the
cross-pollination of Intel and Alpha developers in and of
itself should enrich the IPF architecture. The on-chip
memory controller, interprocessor communications
interfaces, and on-chip routing technology that EV7 brings
to the table should be of particular interest to the IPF
development team. With McKinley a done deal the question
becomes one of when Compaq will be able to impact IPF chip
development. In the meantime, it�ll be interesting to see
how Compaq will execute its strategy before it can fully
impact IPF chip design.

Wildfire, Marvel, then What?

Mongo systems based on CPU Blades and an InfiniBand fabric,
just as Compaq planned to deliver. Well over a year ago,
Compaq�s High Performance Systems Division began designing
a post-Marvel enterprise server that will leverage a
processor �blade� and InfiniBand switched fabric
architecture. (InfiniBand is a new low latency high
bandwidth I/O and interconnect architecture that
incorporates many of the concepts of Compaq�s ServerNet
architecture. Those familiar with the ISSG's forthcoming
IA-32-based QuickBlade server can consider the future IPF
enterprise server to be "QuickBlade on Steroids.") Slated
to debut in the middle of the decade, the server was
designed to be�and still will be�the successor to the next-
generation GS-Series product, the EV7-based Marvel.
(Featuring a modular design that leverages EV7�s �glueless
SMP� on-chip routing and interconnect technology, Marvel
will support up to 64 Alpha EV7 processors and offer
significant improvements in performance, scalability, and
cost of ownership. Prototype Marvel systems using EV7
processors are now running in Compaq�s labs.)


The IPF consolidation poses no adverse impact on the
delivery of Marvel systems or on the development of the
post-Marvel enterprise server. Freed from the burden of
developing and funding Alpha CPUs, Compaq can focus more
resources on system-level enhancements and product
differentiators such as RAS, common management tools,
middleware, advanced partitioning and resource management
capabilities, etc-in short, all the underpinnings for the
firm's long-term Server Utility vision.

Details, We Need Details!

I will be expanding on this and other subjects during the
kickoff session on Sunday night at the Compaq Enterprise
Technical Symposium to be held in Anaheim September 8-14.
For more details please visit http://www.CETS2001.com. I
also routinely cover the Compaq cross currents in my
publication "Shannon Knows Compaq". For more details please
visit http://www.acersoft.com.


