"Jordan Henderson" <jordan@lisa.gemair.com> wrote in message
news:9ibj2h$9nt$1@lisa.gemair.com...
>   What really drove the move from IA16 to IA32 was not customer
>   demand for faster systems, but rather customer demand for
>   the new system software (Windows386, OS/2, Unix) that required
>   IA32.  This is what will drive the move from IA32 to IA64.

No, that isn't true.  386 systems shipped for a long time before Win95
shipped.  Win3.1 included multiple memory managers to support at least three
different methods of mapping more than 16 bit addressing could support; the
one that most systems used was based on 386 memory management, but that just
made the systems cheaper.

The big thing with 386 is the data path width which was 32 bits as opposed
to 16 bits.  That made the 386 substantially faster even when doing
everything in 16 bit mode.

I don't expect to see any demand for new software.  In fact, I'm going to
avoid XP if possible because I expect that I will run into problems keeping
the system running because I change the configuration significantly on my
PC.  I don't want to end up in the situation where I'm calling Microsoft in
three years every few months to get my PC working again after another
configuration change.

Businesses are probably going to find that they can get by without going to
win/office 2k and will likely standardize on what they have today.  The
current economy has caused a lot of companies to delay upgrades and they
will probably find that they can delay for years.

I have friends who are dealing with the aftermath of the dotbomb and they
are telling stories of server farms of a 100 wintel systems that are being
replaced by a pair of Sun or linux systems - it turns out that the wintel
systems were single function systems without storage scalability running
mostly idle.  The costs per month are dropping from $100K per month to $5K
for these server farms.  The guys cleaning up this mess are not going to be
spending a lot of money on expensive new systems when they still have old
stuff to dispose of.




