I hope I understand you correctly. Let me explain a few things:

The console allows you to set up a password, which is entirely
independent
from your UNIX password. After all, it is in effect before any OS is
loaded

To set up the console password, you type
>>> set password
whereupon the console asks you for a password, and a confirmation.
From now
on (after a "set secure" or "init", actually), the console is in
secure mode,
and you can only use the following commands: boot (without
parameters), login,
start, and continue.

I assume that the password was set by somebody else, and you were able
to boot
the OS, but not boot with parameters. I don't know of any way of the
password and secure mode being set up by trying to load other
software.

So you have a machine you can boot, but not do anything else with.
Look up in
your Owners Guide, User Information, Console Reference, or whatever
it's called
for your machine, how to reset the password if you've forgotten it.
This
procedure is different for different models.

For example, for my old DEC 3000 Model 600, I would have to set a
jumper
and enter a certain "deposit" command. For my XP900, I have to type
(at the >>> Prompt) "login", then (at the password prompt) hit the
HALT button,
then press return.

I hope this helps.

Florian

