In article <9iaevf$39o$1@pyrite.mv.net>, Bill Todd <billtodd@foo.mv.com> wrote:
> Contrast the above situation with the move from IA16 to IA32.  That move was
> hardware-technology-driven, not demand-driven:  for many years there were
> virtually *no* 32-bit desktop applications, despite that fact that typical
> 16-bit applications had been standing on their heads for years to make use
> of the megabytes of available physical memory (exactly the reverse of the
> situation today).  Users bought 32-bit hardware mostly because it offered
> better performance (at little or no additional cost) running their 16-bit
> OSs and 16-bit applications.

Actually, there were quite a lot of 32-bit desktop applications. They just
werent running on wintel hardware.

For example, graphics work was largely being done on Macs, and video on Amigas,
because the 68000 could handle large bitmaps efficiently and the 80[2]86
couldn't.

Once Win32 got moving (and the first Win32 apps could run under Win32s on
Windows 3.1), Win32 ports from the 68000 world started showing up. You got
decent perfomance on Photoshop from the Mac, and the Video Toaster software
from the Amiga.

But there aren't really any 64-bit desktop apps. The 64-bit apps are things
like huge databases, and they run on servers.

The only desktop app that I can think of off the top of my head that really
could use that kind of storage is really hefty data mining, stuff that's too
compute intensive to delegate to servers, and how big a market are investment
bankers?

> In sum, while I can imagine that Intel would *like* IA64 to replace IA32 in
> the mass market ASAP, if only to shut out AMD and simplify its own product
> lines, it's hard to imagine that the market will let them.  Am I missing
> something critical here?

In the final analysis, no, I don't think so... in fact I think the situation
is worse than for the 16-32 bit transition because there's no "64-bit Mac"
market for wintel desktops to try and take over.

