job of explaining and convincing
- the management do not want to or are not able to explain the project in
full detail for external reasons (legal, contractual, they don't want the
competition to know etc)

The assumption on comp.arch is always that the truth is the first case. 
My guess is that it is at least as often the second or third case. 
However the interesting situation is, I think, the fourth case, where the
institutional structure of stock-market capitalism results in problems
that would not occur under bank capitalism. I think it would be very
interesting to know if such situations have actually occurred, how severe
the problem was, and how the problem played out.

A somewhat orthogonal issue to all this is, I think, managers that don't
have any guts. Rather than management simply telling CNN-fn that finance
is not a spectator sport and that they basically don't give a fuck about
the fact that this quarter's financials came in 1 cent under analysts'
projections because the larger ten year plan is playing out just fine,
management try to play the media game with day-to-day spin and similar
nonsense. To my eyes this looks like yet another manifestation of that
strange American disease: wanting the whole world---including strangers
like journalists---to love you every hour of every day, and being
tremendously upset when they don't.

Maynard


