It's not clear that the two are related (or mutually exclusive).  We could
avoid the media circus of executions (though I happen to think that being
reminded that they take place has importance) and the build-up of anxiety on
the part of the executee by similarly not revealing the time beforehand.
And there have been enough cases where a decision to execute was in fact
reversed by some mechanism that it's difficult to argue against the appeals
process itself.  In sum, if I were sitting on death row, whether deservedly
(if one believes in capital punishment) or not, I think I'd prefer to be
alive for that decade of appeals - and if that weren't the case, I could
simply choose not to appeal, so suggesting that that opportunity is
'inhumane' seems a stretch.

