I did some looking, and I think you're right.  The -protect_headers and
protect_headers_setup were new in DU 4.0d (1998-1999 timeframe), and an
email conversation I had with a (then-DEC) guy lead me to believe that
short of editing the headers themselves, that was the first "elegant"
way to protect the headers & libraries while compiling for ILP32.  I
misunderstood him by omission.  He never mentioned any other, previous
way of doing it, short of editing system headers, and the pragmas certainly
weren't mentioned where they should have been -- in the compiler man page,
right under the -taso & related options. That notwithstanding, the feature
has been there a long time.

Thanks for the clarification.

