Multiple people have offered as IA64's manifest destiny a near-term
replacement of IA32 in the low-end (desktop) arena, and used the resulting
volume to suggest that IA64 will have unbeatable cost advantages everywhere.
Leaving aside the fact that the cost of the processor(s) is only a small
portion of total system costs (especially outside the low end), Iet's first
investigate the desktop migration question itself.

It's worth remembering that very few current desktop applications would
benefit *at all* from a 64-bit address space.  Of the remainder, most would
perform better if they handled their data explicitly (from disk, or from
Xeon-style extended physical memory) rather than just letting it sprawl
across a larger address space paged sub-optimally by the OS (e.g., game
developers are typically willing to expend such effort for the improved
performance).

And of the even smaller application residue after those are removed, as long
as IA32 systems constitute even a large, let alone a dominant, portion of
the market, developers will when at all feasible tend to create a single
version that will run both there and (perhaps emulated) on IA64.  Even when
a machine is dedicated to running a single application, until some
significant percentage of low-end machines have more than 2 GB of *physical*
memory (or more than 4 GB in cases where the OS doesn't take half the
virtual address space for its own use, or more than 64 GB if Xeon-style
address extensions are used), it will be a very rare desktop-style
application indeed that will see significant advantages in 64-bit
addressing:  things like high-end servers and CAD are the likely
beneficiaries (just as they are today).

Contrast the above situation with the move from IA16 to IA32.  That move was
hardware-technology-driven, not demand-driven:  for many years there were
virtually *no* 32-bit desktop applications, despite that fact that typical
16-bit applications had been standing on their heads for years to make use
of the megabytes of available physical memory (exactly the reverse of the
situation today).  Users bought 32-bit hardware mostly because it offered
better performance (at little or no additional cost) running their 16-bit
OSs and 16-bit applications.

By abandoning hardware upward-compatibility in IA64, Intel has significantly
altered the circumstances from that earlier migration:  IA32 seems likely to
enjoy absolute performance advantages running 32-bit applications for at
least a while, and cost/performance advantages for even longer.  As a
result, the desktop (and even low-end server) IA32 market should continue to
be far larger than the 64-bit market, and developers will continue to create
new applications for the IA32 architecture until it *really* starts to cramp
their style (hence the probability that an IA64-only 'killer app' will light
a migration fire is reduced).

In sum, while I can imagine that Intel would *like* IA64 to replace IA32 in
the mass market ASAP, if only to shut out AMD and simplify its own product
lines, it's hard to imagine that the market will let them.  Am I missing
something critical here?

- bill





