>>>>> "Larry" == Larry Kilgallen <Kilgallen@eisner.decus.org.nospam> writes:
> "Getting rid of" a language-required check is difficult (unless the
> number being converted from something large to byte is known at
> compile time).

It's not more difficult than eliminating array bounds checks.

> Certainly compilers can interleave computational steps with load-store
> steps for CPUs with multiple execution steps.  But if they were not
> interleaving the checks for "fitting" into a bytes, they could be
> doing something else with those CPU resources.

But those checks do not impede data-flow, so they can be done in parallel
with pretty much anything.  Given the amount of unexploited issue-slots in
current processors, the few added instructions tend to cost less than it
would seem at first.  Of course, for embedded systems the situation might
be very different.

I don't want to sound like all those bounds checks come for free, but that
given a good enough optimizer they can often be made cheap enough that it
doesn't make much sense to add special-purpose support for it in the ISA.


	Stefan


