> Kilgallen@eisner.decus.org.nospam (Larry Kilgallen) writes:
> 
>>"Getting rid of" a language-required check is difficult (unless the
>>number being converted from something large to byte is known at
>>compile time).
> 
> But aren't most "byte" stores just stores of "large register holding
> only 8 bits of data as guaranteed by the compiler and runtime" into
> main memory?  Surely ADA doesn't require runtime checks on the runtime
> system?  (I know you put a char in that register just now, but please
> make sure it's still a char?)

Certainly if compile-time analysis is possible, this is not an issue.
Thus the Character datatype is not a problem.  But many programmers
will perform numeric manipulation with type Integer (since it comes
to them naturally) and then store the results into a cell of type
Short_Short_Integer (with conversion) later on.  Certainly the
programmers could do better, but the compiler has to do what they
say.

> Only when the code does some form of type coercion from int to byte you
> will need to do a check; it's by no means certain that a trapping
> store would help you as many cases probably involve reg,reg moves.

While a hardware assist on store would not cover all cases, I thought
the point was to make the check more efficient in software rather than
relieve the compiler of all responsibility for understanding this issue.
I do think most intermediate results (from the viewpoint of the programmer,
not the compiler internals) are of type integer, even when destined to be
stored in something smaller.


