In article <3b4708d3_1@news2.prserv.net>, sad@utk.edu wrote:

> Hi all,
> 
>    I am running a bunch of Tru64 machines (Compaq XP-1000 and ES-40)
> which are used both as compute servers and interactive workstations.
> Alas, it seems too hard for some of the users to (bother to) submit
> background jobs with nice, which makes interactive work unpleasant for
> others.
> 
> Is there a way to get Tru64 to take care of that? I know I can write a
> shell script that parses 'top' putput and renice all jobs which take
> more than n% of CPU, run for more than m minutes and are not from root,
> sys, daemon, ... but before reinventing the wheel I'd rather ask. I
> recall that on AIX there are some simple stanzas for that -- what can I
> do on Tru64? (In case it matters, am running normal security.)
> 
> OS version is 4.0F and up.
> 
>  Cheers,
>           Stefan

This isn't exactly what you want, but you could consider enabling 
autonice, where the system lowers the priority of processes after they 
have consumed a certain amount of CPU time.

It's set by the "autonice", "autonice-time" and "autonice-penalty" 
kernel attributes, which you can change with dxkerneltuner (in the 
"proc" category).

Turn it on by changing the value of autonice from 0 to 1.  autonice-time 
is the amount of CPU time, in seconds, after which autonicing occurs, 
and autonice-penalty is the nice level that's applied.

