To address your comment specifically, when the IBM PC was designed as a
16-bit machine, DEC's top-end 16-bit minicomputers had supported up to 4 MB
of physical memory for close to a decade (I assume other vendors' did as
well), so the utility of memory well beyond 640 KB was not exactly a secret
(which is not to say that this initial limitation did not make sense for
economic reasons at the PC's introduction, simply that anyone who felt it
would be adequate for the long term was not well-acquainted with the rest of
the industry at that time).

So (as I said in my original post) the 16-to-32-bit transition was at least
in large part driven by the awkwardness of developing applications (and
operating systems) on an architecture that supported and made use of far
more physical memory than the virtual address space of its word length could
address without remapping.  And even so, after the 386 appeared in (IIRC)
1986, it took 4 or 5 years before Microsoft came out with an OS that
supported 32-bit applications in any reasonable manner (Win 3.1, I think -
and that was still a 16-bit OS) and more time after that before 32-bit
applications became at all common (in fact, I'm not sure they could have
been considered very common before Win95 appeared).

Contrast that time, a time when widening the word size had significant
benefits for the systems and applications that already existed (and yet it
*still* took half a decade for them even to begin to take advantage of it)
with today, when virtually all desktop applications run fat and happy in a
32-bit address space and nothing but high-end servers and workstations
(certainly not desktop units) sports anywhere nearly as much physical memory
as an application can map directly using 32 bits.

So while your comment does have some amusement value, I fail to see any
actual pertinence.

- bill

