I understand.  It was usually the hardware and the detection was pretty
primitive given the costs and state of hardware then.  I remember one
instance where a sense amp for one of the register groups was marginal.
Some of the time it would work, but depending on the data pattern, it would
silently write incorrect results into an A register.  This result was used
in future calculations and things went down hill fast, leading to a system
crash.  I remember working with the CE, patching in a loop of the three
instruction sequence that we found from the dumps that had failed and
getting them to fail in fraction of a second. They replaced one card in the
CPU and everything got better.

I am not blaiming the design.  It was doing the best it could within the
constraints of the hardware of the day.


Let me also clarify that it was a symmetrical system in that each CPU was
hardware identical and was treated as an equivalent resource by the OS.
When I first saw SMP systems like the Sequent Balance, I was very
comfortable with their overall architecture as they were similar. (Of
course, on a different scale)  I should also say that cache coherence was
not a problem as the CPUs had no cache!



