> The reason for not implementing byte/word ops was to avoid having to do a
> read/modify/write operation to update memory.  To write a byte into memory,
> you must read the longword containing the byte, do any required ECC
> corrections, insert the byte, compute a new ECC, and then update memory.
> This could be done in the memory controller which was the implementation
> used on VAX systems, or it could be done in the CPU which would require the
> CPU reading the longword from memory and writing the updated longword back.
> 
> At the time, writeback cache was not standard.
> 
> Btw, Dick Sites wanted to make a quadword (64 bits) be the smallest memory
> transaction.
> 
> The idea was that if you were writing memory, say as part of a bcopy, the
> limiting factor would be the memory read + memory write speed which could be
> increased by multiple memory banks.
> 
> What this did not consider was that the transaction unit between the CPU and
> memory would shift to a cache line.  Once the cache line is the unit of
> transaction and then it becomes writeback, the assumptions that drove that
> particular design choice became suboptimal; in the above bcopy, the CPU
> reads the cache line in order to write the first portion of the cache line.
> 
> There were two architectural changes made at almost the same time to correct
> this incorrect assumption:  The byte/word memory ops were added AND an
> instruction was added that hints that an entire cache line will be written.
> This allows the CPU to do the bcopy without reading the destination memory.
