"Paul DeMone" <pdemone@igs.net> wrote in message
news:3B471A6C.EE8715F5@igs.net...
> > > SPARC always had 8 and 16 bit loads and stores.
> >
> > It is interesting that PowerPC does not have a load or store signed byte
>
> What's the difference between a signed and unsigned store?

What's the difference between a signed and unsigned load?

Even C recognizes that fetching/storing a data value, converting to another
type, and using the data are three operations.  Combining the conversion and
memory operation into one instruction is at best an optimization, but in
probably makes life a lot more difficult for the CPU implementer with little
value.

Sign extended byte loads doesn't eliminate the requirement for byte
arithmetic operations, so its necessary to have byte to other type
conversions even with load signed byte.

If PowerPC has an instruction that converts from 64 bits to 8 bits in signed
and unsigned formats that signals in some fashion a conversion error, then
combining the conversion and store into one operation would be very
complicated.




