Whew!  I worried there for a second because that's how I read
what you wrote :-).

> ..It's just certain that if you are using the CPU and memory for
>whatever thing at that time, some work will be lost and it may be
>the same as if the entire system was lost.  In other words, the
>application running on te cluster must also be tolerant of the failure
>of parts of it, just as the cluster's kernel is.

Sure.  From my aged viewpoint, it is the monitor's business
to keep track of its data and it's the application's business
to keep track of its data and shouldn't care where it ran.
Once an app gets involved at the monitor level, you need
to look at the system as a stand-alone system dedicated to
that one user.  No longer can you service many users.

I know I'm not saying this well, but I am trying to point
out how OS philosophies differ and why they differ.

/BAH

