...

> Most of the high level languages have support for ISAM files built into
them.
> It is only when using C (C++ ?) that you are forced into using FAB/RAB
etc.

Unfortunately,

1.  While the languages help make doing simple operations simple, RMS
Indexed Files have a fair amount of inherent complexity (not all of which is
necessary) that needs to be understood in order to do even simple operations
*efficiently*.

2.  Most of the languages provide some means of getting at the less common
RMS options - via use of utilities if nothing else - which means that even
though one may not need them for most of one's work, they're still there in
the documentation (and thus things you tend to need to read about just to
know that you don't need them).

>
> I don't really see that even at the FAB/RAB etc level RMS is any more
> complex than say writing X-windows programs or any number of other
> applications where you need to call specialised functions.

Which is to say that RMS isn't any more complex than a lot of other complex
areas.  Most people prefer to avoid *unnecessary* complexity in their work
even if they're capable of dealing with it where they must:  that's one of
the attractions of Unix programing (as distinct from the ridiculous
interfaces to Unix utility programs).

- bill

