Greg Pfister wrote:
[snip]
> Otherwise... It's clearly time for a re-post of what I wrote
> about the meaning of "symmetric" a while ago. Only I can't find a
> copy. Dang.

A hearty Thanks! to Natarajan Krishaswami, who e-mailed me a
copy. Looking at it anew, it doesn't seem quite as directly
applicable as I thought. But it's still likely helpful in this
context.

Here 'tis:

On 2000-09-21, Greg Pfister <pfister@us.ibm.com> wrote:
> "John R. Mashey" wrote:
> [much good stuff snipped]
> >
> > SUMMARY: it is easy to get confused with terminology, and there is no
> > final arbiter of official meaning, but once again:
> >         NUMAs can be symmetric (and the most well-known ones are so,
> >         with symmetric hardware, and symemtric OS (albeit with varying degrees
> >         of kernel parallelism).  Whether or not a symmetric NUMA is
> >         an "SMP" depends on one's choice of meaning for SMP, but it is
> >         still symmetric.
>
> John,
>
> As you indicate, there's no final arbiter. But I think there's a
> definite sense in which NUMA can be said to not be symmetric --
> or asymmetric, even with the same definition of the word
> "symmetric." The argument I make is somewhat along the lines of
> your hardware/software distinction, but I think a little more
> precise.
>
> The word "symmetric" means "looks the same from all positions on
> an axis of symmetry." At least that's the definition I've always
> used; I can't quote an Authority, but I could probably look in a
> dictionary for something meaning that.
>
> A key issue then is: What's the axis of symmetry you're using?
> Things can be symmetric on one axis, and assymmetric on others.
>
> For me, the points on the axis for an SMP are the processors. So
> the symmetry test is how they "see" the system.
>
> In a traditional UMA SMP, if you figuratively stand on a
> processor and "look" at the rest of the system, everything looks
> (behaves) identical: Everybody can do the same I/O functions,
> everybody can get at the same range of memory with identical
> characteristics. The latency characteristics of any given memory
> location will vary over time, depending on that location's
> residence in cache(s), but the variation is the same for all
> processors so symmetry is maintained. So an UMA SMP is indeed
> symmetric along the processor axis.
>
> That's not true of NUMA. A the same memory location X will have
> permanently different access characteristics from different
> processors depending on where X's home location is: In the same
> physical module as the processor, or in a remote module. I/O has
> similar characteristics. So NUMA is assymetric along the axis of
> processors.
>
> That doesn't mean it's not symmetric along other axes, like
> whether any processor can do any given new piece of work.
>
> So, do I think a NUMA system is an SMP? No. But do I call NUMA
> systems SMPs? Yes. The reason is completely unrelated to the
> technical discussion above.
>
> I happen to think that NUMA-ish-ness is a characteristic that
> will eventually pervade all large shared-memory multiprocessor
> systems to a varying, but ultimately increasing, extent.
> Everybody will ultimately have to just deal with that, like they
> have to deal with the fact that SMPs don't run single-thread code
> faster, and programs with very poor locality of reference run
> slowly on just about everything nowadays.
>
> That being the case, since "SMP" is the term which the great mass
> of of the most important class of people (customers!!!) now uses
> when they mean "shared-memory multiprocessor system," I've
> reluctantly come to accept SMP as something that, while literally
> wrong for at least my preferred choice of symmetry axis,
> encompasses NUMA. That's just the way the language is currently
> evolving, like it or not. Lots of words have changed meaning,
> even reversed their meaning, over time. Maybe someday everybody
> will use a term other than SMP, one that's arguably "more
> correct" by some definition, but right now I find that the best
> term to use to successfully communicate with the most important
> people is "SMP." So I use it.
>
> There will still be continuing arguments about this with
> technical sticklers, but it will be just as meaningful as arguing
> with the people who insist at every possible opportunity that the
> new century hasn't started yet. (And who, by the way and IMHO,
> are wrong, not because of numeric arguments -- those are right --
> but because the monk who re-zeroed the calender long ago got it
> wrong.)
>
> Greg Pfister
> <not necessarily my employer's opinion>


