Pio Baettig <baettig@hotmail.com> wrote:
> How do I have to proceed to install them both, each on one of the disks?

   you install linux on one disk and tru64 on the other disk.

> Can Tru64 read Linux Partitions and vice-versa?

   linux can read tru64-partitions (via a loadable module or you
   can compile this feature in the linux-kernel).

   i don't know about tru64.

> As far as I have understood by now (reading the SRM howto at
> http://www.alphalinux.org/faq/x661.html ), I have to partition the disks
> using the Tru64 utility and to install Tru64 first, than to install Linux
> without repartitioning the disks.

   that's it! you issue the boot-command from srm with the appropriate
   parameters. you might boot tru64 by typing "boot dka1 -fl A" and boot
   linux by typing "boot dka2 -fi 2/vmlinux -fl 'root=...'".

   you use the same boot command with different partitions and different
   boot-options (flags).

> [...] now, I can boot linux with only typing ">>>boot")

   that works because you have the boot-parameters (bootdef_dev and
   boot_osflags) set. do a "show boot*" to see those parameters.

> What size should the swap-partitions have?

   i would set them to 256 mb.

