Larry Kilgallen wrote:
> 
> In article <fyt07.406522$eK2.82328712@news4.rdc1.on.home.com>, "Yousuf Khan" <ykhan@nospam.home.com.spam> writes:
> > "Larry Kilgallen" <Kilgallen@eisner.decus.org.nospam> wrote in message
> > news:xu1946w16jvw@eisner.encompasserve.org...
> >> > Sun, HP, and Veritas would beg to differ with you.
> >>
> >> Sun and HP are late to the game and trying to make excuses.
> >
> > I'm afraid not, it truly is a form of clustering, whether it fits into your
> > narrow definition or not.
> >
> >> Another way to configure failover.  Certainly not clustering,
> >> no matter what Veritas uses for their product name.
> >
> > Certainly is, your narrow view needs broadening.
> 
> So far as I know, the term was first used by VMS about 1985,
> for what they offered then (not materially different from today
> with regard to the capabilities under discussion).
> 
> Was there a prior use of "cluster" in this context ?
> 
> If not, then lesser copycat efforts are not really clusters.

I definitely credit DEC with coining the term "cluster." The
first public use of the term I've found was in: Nancy P.
Kronenberg, Henry M. Levy, and William D. Strecker. "Vaxclusters:
A Closely-Coupled Distributed System." ACM Transactions on
Computer Systems, 4(3):130�146, May 1986.

That doesn't mean they invented the *concept* of clustering. I'd
put that way back sometime in the early 60s or even 50s, when
some unknown customer first said "We really need a spare computer
if we're going to be sure of doing X on time. Probably should
make some use of it in the meantime." There were groups of
mainframes being used as units with JES (Job Entry System)
fanning out the work appropriately to them back in the 70s,
probably pre-dating VMS itself.

Of course, if your personal definition of "cluster" is
"VMSCluster," and nothing else need apply, you're hardly likely
to agree with the above. You're of course free to use the term as
you want, but I think holding onto that definition will hinder
your communication with the rest of the world.

Greg Pfister


