In article <9dbf5b33.0107030311.1444fdf3@posting.google.com>, dionys@tip.nl (Dionys) writes:

>Since a few days I am taking care of an Digital Unix/tru64 machine.
>But I need to know what kind of hardware is inn the inside of that
>machine. Since nobody knows what kind of HW is in it and have no clue
>of what I am talking about I cannot proceed with that task. Digital
>Unix/tru64 is new for me and Linux commandos do not work.

First, it would help to know what version of the operating system
you're running.  V5 significantly changed the way some things work
and this is relevent in providing information about which commands
to use.  For example, hwmgr(8) won't be on a V4 system.

Next, you should find out what sort of system you have.  Various
versions of DEC OSF/1, Digital UNIX and Tru64 UNIX have on systems
ranging from EISA based workstations and small servers to large
systems with 32 CPUs, double digit gigabytes of memory and multi-
digit terabytes of disk space.  The name and model number of the
system offers a lot of clues about the sort things you're likely
to find.

The boot messages offer a lot of information about what is on a
system and what type of system it is.  These are written to the
binary error log and /var/adm/messages.  Some systems write more
data to the error log on boot, which can be read with DECevent;
FRU data.  DECevent may not be able to interpret all error log
formats, but where it doesn't Compaq Analyze may be able to.

The boot listings will show you what I/O adapters you have and
how they're organized inside the box.  It is possible to dynamically
change what is on some I/O busses (StorageWorks enclosures on SCSI
for example), so what was present when the system booted is not
necessarily what is present "now".  The scu(8) command will let
you see what the SCSI driver thinks is present.

On the systems that have it, sys_check collects a lot of configuration
information that may be useful.

