Bill, with one exception I agree with all you said here,
particularly with regard to fault tolerance / high avaialability.
SMPs are definitely not architecturally fault-tolerant, and they
*will* die completely if only one processor bites the dust. Ditto
NUMA systems.

The exception:

It's possible for I/O to be physically attached just to one
physical unit (processor(s) and/or memory) with its own I/O bus,
making it available to others via the memory hierarchy. E.g., if
the bus is memory-mapped, all the busses are in the memory space
of all the processors, no matter which unit the bus is in. 

It's still the case then that every processor has the same
"logical" capabilities; just one of them does it a tad faster
than others. This is all "SMP" means, anyway. The software may or
may not be optimized to deal with the performance asymmetries.

Otherwise... It's clearly time for a re-post of what I wrote
about the meaning of "symmetric" a while ago. Only I can't find a
copy. Dang.

Greg Pfister

