news:9hj3ch$qmi$1@pyrite.mv.net...
> That's funny, I don't find that easy at all to think of any
> development-related decisions an engineer could have been in a position to
> have made that would have had a noticeably negative effect on Alpha's
market
> share.  Lack-luster funding for the hardware cornerstone (or later, under
> Compaq, at least *one* hardware cornerstone) of the corporation?  It would

I will argue that there was one decision that probably had a significant
impact on market share.

And Dick Sites admitted that it was a mistake.

In order to achieve the maximum possible speed up in clock rate, there was a
decision to avoid read/modify/write sequences, either in the CPU or in the
memory system.  This decision was made when writeback caches were
problematic for multiprocessor systems.

The result was that Alpha had no byte/word memory ops.

At the time, the target operating systems were unix and VMS.  For unix, the
arguement was that customers had no problem recompiling and modifying code
for a new system which offered cost performance benefits.  And VMS, well,
performance wasn't an issue, besides VMS will die soon, or wasn't important,
la dee da.

Well, byte/word ops were a problem for unix, especially for I/O devices and
graphic framebuffers.  And the problems became instantiously worse when PCI
was adopted over XMI and Turbochannel.

Then, NT was going to be targeted at Alpha.

The idea of changing windows code to get around the lack of word/byte ops
quickly went out the window.  Trying to make the compilers do the right
thing proved to be either expensive or very costly.  It was impossible to
tell which code was potentially running in a multithreaded environment and
needed a complex load locked, modify, store conditional loop or merly a
simple load, modify, store.

EV4 was already out, EV5 was too far along to change, and EV56 could only
add word/byte ops to memory, but would require system redesigns to allow for
their use for I/O.

NT was cancelled just as word/byte ops became fully available in systems.
With NT, it might have been possible to say 64 bit NT requires EV6 and above
with generous trade-in programs for the existing Alpha NT customer base if
that would encourage MS to put more support behind it.  But early EV6
problems seen with NT caused MS to pull back from supporting EV6 systems,
waiting for Ev67 or later passes of EV6.  And then, everything fell
apart....

This was a significant design error which was corrected.  This is similar to
the serious design error in VAX which was its high complexity for little
purpose; that was also corrected by subsetting the architecture.

It seems to me that IA64 is flawed as well with the kitchen sink worth of
features and no clear evidence that they can be effectively used to overcome
the time to market and implementation penalties that the architecture
requires.

Remember, one of the reasons that the VAX was bad was that it was complex
and to achieve the same peformance as a RISC chip in the same process and
design methodology would required a more aggressive design and an extra
year.

We I think that we can see a fair comparison with EV7 against McKinley.
Both started design at about the same time; both will be released about the
same time; both will use similar chip processes.  And with the number of
resources devoted to McKinley relative to EV7, it either matches or betters
the performance with a few months, or else the architecture bears
responsibility, unless someone is going to stand up at say they screwed up.




