funded DEC, but as the General's health declined, Wall Street started
driving more and more of the business decisions.  To understand what was
going on, read The Innovator's Dilemma.  The problem with DEC was more than
missing the "PC revolution".  The problem was that the diverse directions
that engineers wanted to take the company were stifled.  Those of us working
on PDP-11 software wanted to drive down market, but we were inhibited from
doing so for two reasons:
    1) we would reduce the margins and/or gross revenue
         (Even with the same gross margin, a lower gross revenue would
result
         in lower service costs which would reduce gross and net revenues in
         that part of the company, unless volume increased beyond
expectation.)
    2) we would reduce the penetration of VAX/VMS by competing with it

Another factor was the one egg, one basket idea that said VAX/VMS was it
which then lead to the VAX/VMS vs unix battle.  Until VAX/VMS, DEC had had
multiple overlapping products. KO allowed/encouraged some degree of
competition.  For an engineer, keeping several options open is always a good
idea.  The overlapping products ended up resulting in complex alliances
forming - the compiler groups ended up figuring out how to have a single
compiler for multiple operating systems which lead to lots of code being
shared between RT, RSX, and RSTS.

But Wall Street, the MBAs, looked at this as inefficiency.

For the MBA the right way to manage the business is:
    Chose only the successful product.
    The only product you have must be the successful product, even if you
sell just one





