...

> >My understanding is that modern SMP systems not only do not guarantee the
> >lack of any single point of failure (that's something close to a
> >triple-negative, I think...) but in most cases don't tolerate
> >the failure of
> >any one of their CPUs:
>
> Ooops.  We are talking about two vastly different things.  The
> implementation, which was called and sold as SMP, could
> tolerate a CPU failure (with the exception of the last
> CPU failing).  It also provided the system operator a way
> to gracefully remove a CPU and the other gear from the system
> without interrupting computing service to the hundreds of
> users that were logged in.
>
> OSes don't do this anymore?

Some do (the 'gracefully remove' part, anyway).  VMS may be one of the few
(I strongly suspect S/390 is another, and possibly Solaris) where you can
migrate processes away from a running CPU and then remove its board without
affecting the running system.

Tolerating a CPU failure is harder, unless the box is 'hard-partitioned'
into several effectively independent systems (though they may communicate
through specific sections of shared memory).  The problem is that portions
of the system, as well as the application, may have been running on that CPU
and been left in some inconsistent state.

Failing memory boards face similar problems in non-hard-partitioned
configurations.  I think I recall that S/390 (and quite possibly its
predecessors) attempts to deal with them gracefully (whether it can do so
100% of the time I don't know), but I don't know if others do:  it's very
hard to know what system-critical data might have resided in the failed
area.

>
>
> > ...modern SMP is mostly about aggregating the
> >performance of multiple CPUs, not about availability.
>
> I'd tend to call that parallel processing...but lets not
> go down that semantic thread drift...I don't really want
> to argue about definitions.  I do like to argue about concepts
> and what kinds of computing should be out there ;-))).

I think most people nowadays think of SMP as being much more tightly-coupled
than 'parallel processing' (that's not meant to be an argument, but this
discussion does seem to have its roots in differing definitions, and it's
difficult to talk about things at all until you've agreed at least to some
degree on what the words mean).

Modern SMP seems to be aimed at parallelizing at relatively fine grain -
e.g., concurrently running multiple threads in the same process or the
system itself, rather than, say, running different processes on each
processor.  There are a lot of applications that benefit greatly from this,
since most systems allow in-process multi-threading to execute significantly
more efficiently than inter-process cooperation can (the latter usually
requiring kernel interaction at synchronization points - though some systems
would allow you to roll your own in shared memory more efficiently but with
noticeably more coding effort - and making it difficult to avoid scheduling
artifacts).

> This is because I am appalled at the crap that is out there.
>
> >
> >Corrections to the above would be most welcome:  I
> >wouldn't want to cling to
> >a major misunderstanding in this area any more
> >than I suspect you would.
>
> Right.  :-)  I hadn't realized that computing availability per
> system seems to have disappeared.
>
> When you guys do these designs, are you really assuming that
> hardware never fails?

The penalty modern SMP pays for the tighter coupling described above is
reliance on all the processors to keep running.  But hardware *is* pretty
reliable these days, so the MBTF isn't at all bad - and other mechanisms
(hard-partitioning within a single unit, clustering between units,
fully-redundant lock-step hardware) are used to increase availability to
higher levels when desired.  There are arguable benefits to partitioning the
problems in this manner:  people can buy performance without paying for
availability they don't need, and vice versa.

