peter@abbnm.com (Peter da Silva) wrote in message news:<9hmrcl$1oa@web.nmti.com>...

[re: P4 performance]
> 
> And given that it's had more money and more smart people applied
> to making it run fast than, well, probably every other CPU that's been
> on the market in the past decade (and not just from intel)... given
> that, no, I don't think it performs all that well.

Well, its true that it doesn't run *existing* code as well as a P3
clocked up to equivalent speed, but examples of code optimized for the
P4 have been *amazingly* fast.  Saying the P4 doesn't perform well isn't
very fair IMO.  Saying "it runs old code slower than an Athlon" is a 
little better.

As an example, look at George Woltman's Prime95, which is a Lucas-Lehmer 
test implementation, and generally considered an excellent example of 
highly efficient x86 assembly.  By re-coding parts of the program, he
*tripled* the speed of the code on P4 versus the old P3-tuned code.  A
P4 1.4GHz runs the P4-specific code more than 2x as fast as a 1.33GHz 
Athlon running the P3/Athlon-optimized code.  And this is just his first
round of optimizations for P4, whereas he's been tuning the P3 code for
quite some time now.  

see http://www.mersenne.org/bench.htm for hard numbers.

Of course, this doesn't *prove* that the P4 is a marvel of CPU design,
but it does show that it *is* capable of some impressive performance
when running appropriate code.  YMMV.

I'd set follow-ups elsewhere, but the google posting interface isn't
very sophisticated.

