>is "dd" realy supported by compaq (Tru64), to clone a system disk ?

Before V5 the answer would be "of course".  Through the end of
V4, blocks were blocks.  I don't have direct experience with
it, but apparently something in V5 remembers which disk was
the system disk (more than just a line in /etc/fstab or a link
under /etc/fdmns).  This limits the usefulness of all the classic
methods of making a system disk clone.

>What about BBRs (Bad Block Replacement) - Information? Are
>those information copied to the destination, too?

I think there are three cases of interest:

1.  A bad block is still bad.

2.  A bad block has been replaced by a good block and correct
    data copied to it.

3.  A bad block has been replaced by a good block and the
    correct data wasn't available.

Left to itself, the operating system won't replace a bad block
if it can't get a good copy of the data.  Poorly behaved I/O
subsystems may replace the bad block for you and lose track
of the fact that they couldn't get good data.  Well behaved
subsystems will keep track and will probably indicate the block
is still bad when read.  Using the normal options, dd(1) will
fail in the this case with an I/O error.

So, while it is possible to lose track of the fact that a block doesn't
contain the data written to it originally, you'll have to work at it.

>If yes, this would be
>an unpredictable behavior. The original information on the target disk
>might be overwritten.
>
>greetings , Mike


>> dd if=/dev/rrz0 of=/dev/rrz5 bs=<as much as possible>

This example is reasonable for V4 and earlier, though I don't
think dd(1) will fill in the partition letter.

