> Now, there are two separate meanings for clustering. There's the
> load-balancing type clustering, where multiple nodes run the same
> applications and share tasks with each other simultaneously to work on
> bigger workloads. Then there is the failover type clustering, where it
> simply a high-availability option. Which one is cited here?

There are a great many more than 2 meanings of 'clustering'.  Greg Pfister
wrote the book 'In Search of Clusters' on the subject (get the second
edition if you look it up - unless he's gotten a third out by now).

My guess is that the kind of clustering Terry's referring to involves both
high-availability and scalability, since that's what TruClusters tend to
support.  Though HP/UX may offer some form of clustering as well, possibly
based on the Veritas software.

