> > > as part of installing the same operating system, patches and software on 16
> > > workstations, I did all the work on one workstation and was hoping to duplicate
> > > the disk contents on to the other workstations. I dumped / and /usr  partitions
> > > on to an external disk. I then restored those partitions on to the internal
> > > disk of another workstation, but it would not boot after that. It would not recognize the devices that represent the disk
> > partitions. Is there a
> > > command to set the boot sector?
> >
> > Yes, look at the disklabel command From the man pages for 4.0F
> > -------
> > -t ufs|advfs| cdfs
> >  Specifies which type of the local file system the boot blocks
> >  describe, UFS, AdvFS, or CDFS.
> > -------
> > BTW on version 5 systems you may have some problems with this approach since the
> > WWID's of the disks will have changed.
> > I haven't come up with a consistent way of solving this problem and it is hampering
> > my DR procedure somewhat so if anyone has any ideas about cloning a V5 system
> > onto new hardware I'd love to hear it.
> > Adam
>
> the disklabel command  is not working for me:
>
> 69 Rot@congo /#hwmgr -view devices
>  HWID: Device Name          Mfg      Model            Location
>  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
>     4: /dev/kevm
>    30: /dev/disk/dsk0c      DEC      RZ28D    (C) DEC bus-0-targ-0-lun-0
>    31: /dev/disk/cdrom0c    DEC      RRD46   (C) DEC  bus-0-targ-4-lun-0
>    32: /dev/disk/dsk1c      SEAGATE  ST39173N         bus-0-targ-5-lun-0
>    33: /dev/disk/dsk2c      SEAGATE  ST318417N        bus-0-targ-3-lun-0
> 70 Rot@congo /#disklabel -r RZ28DB
> disklabel: RZ28DB: No such file or directory
> 71 Rot@congo /#disklabel -r rz28db
> disklabel: rz28db: No such file or directory
> 72 Rot@congo /#uname -a
> OSF1 congo.EECS.Berkeley.EDU V5.1 732 alpha alpha

OK The first problem you have is that the CDrom Drive and the first
disk in the system (The rz28D) are both set to scsi id 4. This is a
hardware problem, shut the machine down and change some jumpers.

Next... The commands you tried were wrong. You need to reference
the disk as /dev/disk/dsk0c (The device special file) not RZ28D (The
type of disk).

Also as I said using dd to copy version 5 system disks will cause you
problems. Your best bet for duplicating installs on a V5 system is to
use the 'clone' install feature (Look at the advanced installation guide
on the doc's CD).

Hope this helps
Adam
PS Disklabel can destroy data if you don't know what you are doing
or don't have a good backup of the disk then don't play with it.





