 I have a real problem with statements like this. First it assumes
 some trajectory for IA64 performance. Presumably that would come
 from Intel and would be so highly NDA that even Alpha proponents
 within Compaq may not have seen it. It reminds me how Dod and the
 defense industry can make claims about a program and dismiss any
 criticism saying the critics don't have the necessary classified
 data to judge the program. Then it assumes that Intel can meet
 its performance. Granted, Alpha has had its shares of slips since
 the EV5 days. The big difference is Intel's IA64 performance claims
 require chips to meet schedule and clock rates AND EPIC compiler
 technology has to reach certain levels of efficacy that clearly no
 one knows how to achieve today. The IA64 program looks to me as if
 NASA went all out on the Apollo program in the 60's without a clue
 of how to build the command module's heat shield hoping for some
 breakthrough to occur before the first manned flights.

 My second issue is the people judging the performance of Alpha
 down the road also hold the purse strings. To a large extent the
 semi technology lag Alpha faces with respect to IA64 is a question
 of how well financed the group is. That's because with a fabless
 model you pay through the nose for early access to new technologies.
 Another factor is that you need a large enough design team to have
 a degree of concurrency to hit each new semi technology node running.
 If the people WAG-ing future future Alpha performance are the ones
 looking for excuses to kill it then there are all kinds of games
 that they can play to make Alpha performance vs time look worse
 enough to justify their desire to kill it( Another shrink? you just
 had a shrink three or four years ago. You profligate bastards!)

 I have real doubts that IA64 performance would have reached parity
 with a reasonably funded Alpha. I guess we will never know because
 Compaq has killed development of the first new Alpha processor core
 (EV8) since the EV6 was developed 5 or 6 years ago. What is that
  old saying about surgeons burying their mistakes?


