|> 
|> I think not.  I believe lack of acceptance by other system vendors is more
|> important.  That is an area where IBM does not do so well with PowerPC
|> either.  Apple is the main adherent, but they have the protection of the
|> Motorola second-source (actually, first-source).  That is much stronger
|> (with Motorola doing more Macintosh-friendly designs), that the API/Samsung
|> work.
|> 
|> I believe that system vendors are reluctant to base their designs on a
|> chip from someone who competes with them in the system vendor space.
|> Smaller companies do not have so much choice, of course.

While that is true, it is clear that the primary cause of Alpha's
demise is mismanagement.  If Compaq wanted to close down Alpha,
they would have done better to do so explicitly - and two years
earlier.  If they wanted to sell it, they should have done so
when it was still a going concern - i.e. two years earlier.  If
they wanted to try to make a success of it, they should have done
so properly - and two years ago.

There were a LOT of customers who wanted Alphas for preference,
and a fair number more who could have been persuaded.  Easily a
million CPUs a year, and that's without breaking into new markets.
But Compaq turned a system with a factor of 2 DELIVERED performance
advantage (and a factor of 4, peak) into just another also ran.


