<jmfbahciv@aol.com> wrote ...
> But it's just common sense.  Take two architects, one hardware
> and the other software.  To be architects, they have a few
> things in common.  1.  They don't suffer fools at all.  2.
> Most of the time they know better than anybody else.

Actually, that latter is usually the sign of a _bad_ architect.

Let's recall that computer architecture is a contract between
the hardware and the software; and that a commercially
successful architecture has to meet the customer's needs.

So microprocessor architecture involves the comprehension
of everything from OS and program structure and behavior,
down through compiler operation, then across to logic design,
tool chain capabilities, validation requirements, package design,
factory product flow and semiconductor fabrication process
parameters.  It even needs a lot of marketing knowledge: what
does the customer think they need, what will they really need in
the time frame the product will come out in, and what will sell.

I've yet to meet a single person that understands all of this
(and the bits in between) well enough to not need the
knowledge of other people "most of the time", the exact opposite
of what you claim is true for "architects".  I do, however, know some
people that know an incredible amount about the bits and pieces.
--
