jmfbahciv@aol.com wrote:
> In order for each to be able to critique the other's ideas,
> they really, really, really do have know that the other guy/gal
> isn't an idiot.  It is not efficient to determine this via
> a net link.

On the other hand, it is very efficient to determine that the other one
is an idiot with a net link ;-). Therefore, I disagree. The two
principal developers of Gforth (that's Anton Ertl and I) did not meet
physically for the first four years of Gforth's development - we had a
few IRC and talk conversions, but almost all conversion was via e-mail.
Then we met at a Forth conference.

A lot of ideas don't look as good as they appeared when you start to
write them down. Therefore, the "write them down" filter of e-mail
communication is already valuable. Archived communication is also
helpful (as well as archived source changes).

Since Gforth is not the only free software project that is developed
with discussions mostly done by e-mail, and even much larger projects
like Linux work essentially the same way, I think the need of physically
present discussion is more for idiots who can't e-mail. I sometimes get
e-mail responses like "I can't give you the comments on your
document/information you requested; a personal meeting is necessary";
and I always sort these people instantly into the "idiot" folder.

Other than meeting with idiots, personal relationships are helped by
physical presence (the stronger the personal aspect, the more). But I
rarely want to date with cow-orkers ;-).

-- 
Bernd Paysan
