Being "the fastest" was Alpha's key differentiator. Alpha sells about 100K
units per year, so perhaps ~250K microprocessors are fabbed (mix of small
and large systems). If it costs $250M per year to maintain the development
of a CPU that has a run rate of 250K, the development costs alone add $1K to
the unit price of each Alpha chip. Perhaps an oversimplification, but do the
math.

>
> Q is putting its entire high-end business at risk here, for a couple
> of percent performance increase?

Q apparently felt the ongoing expense of Alpha development posed more of a
risk than does the adoption if IPF. The post-Marvel fabric-based system is
processor-neutral, so there should be no technical impact from the decision.

>
> This isn't a case of MBA's vs. Engineers making business decsions,
> what we have here is a ship of fools.
>

This decision supposedly came out of Engineering, who apparently looked at
spreadsheets and saw the writing on the wall. And if you go back more than a
decade to the DECline of Digital, note that decisions were driven almost
exclusively by (an) engineer(s).





